Dialogue: 0,0:00:00.97,0:00:03.94,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}This needle has pointed\Nnorth your whole life.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:04.37,0:00:06.90,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}But the seafloor remembers further back.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:07.26,0:00:09.45,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}North and south have swapped.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:09.59,0:00:12.80,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Again and again, with no schedule.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:13.54,0:00:17.00,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}That needle follows a {\c&H3468EB&}field{\c&HF7F4F2&}\Nthat wraps the whole planet.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:17.37,0:00:18.80,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}It feels permanent.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:19.22,0:00:20.42,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}It is not.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:21.07,0:00:24.50,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}At ocean ridges, fresh\Nlava cools into rock.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:24.91,0:00:28.36,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Tiny magnetic grains lock\Nin the {\c&H3468EB&}field's{\c&HF7F4F2&} direction.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:28.75,0:00:32.99,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}The seafloor spreads,\Nand each flip starts a new stripe.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:33.31,0:00:38.94,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Five million years, about twenty\Nflips, and wildly uneven gaps.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:39.87,0:00:41.53,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}So, two questions.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:41.94,0:00:43.58,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}When will it flip next?
Dialogue: 0,0:00:43.67,0:00:45.36,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}And {\c&H00A1ED&}how often{\c&HF7F4F2&} does it flip?
Dialogue: 0,0:00:45.97,0:00:47.59,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Which one can we answer?
Dialogue: 0,0:00:49.83,0:00:54.40,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}In a simple computer model of a\Nflipping {\c&H3468EB&}field{\c&HF7F4F2&}, the first is hopeless.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:54.56,0:00:56.18,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}The second is not.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:56.92,0:01:00.52,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Finding out why takes us\Nthrough liquid metal and chaos.
Dialogue: 0,0:01:00.73,0:01:04.71,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Then to {\c&HF09CB4&}machines{\c&HF7F4F2&} that dream up\Nsnapshots of magnetic fields.
Dialogue: 0,0:01:05.20,0:01:09.92,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}They must obey some laws exactly,\Nand others only on average.
Dialogue: 0,0:01:11.17,0:01:13.18,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}How often does the {\c&H3468EB&}field{\c&HF7F4F2&} flip?
Dialogue: 0,0:01:13.42,0:01:18.69,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}A learner's map of the MilaDy project,\Nfrom a compass to today's research.
Dialogue: 0,0:01:21.24,0:01:23.78,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Should the first\Nsimulations target {\c&H00A1ED&}how often{\c&HF7F4F2&}
Dialogue: 0,0:01:23.86,0:01:27.52,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}the field flips, or the warning\Nsigns just before a flip?
Dialogue: 0,0:01:32.57,0:01:35.52,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Is there a giant magnet\Nhidden inside the Earth?
Dialogue: 0,0:01:36.10,0:01:40.08,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Deep down, the outer core\Nis an ocean of liquid iron.
Dialogue: 0,0:01:40.30,0:01:45.47,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Left alone, a {\c&H3468EB&}field{\c&HF7F4F2&} there would fade\Naway within tens of thousands of years.
Dialogue: 0,0:01:45.70,0:01:48.92,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Yet Earth's field is\Nseveral billion years old.
Dialogue: 0,0:01:49.24,0:01:52.96,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}So the moving iron must keep\Nmaking it, all the time.
Dialogue: 0,0:01:54.00,0:01:58.11,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Take a bar magnet: north at\None end, south at the other.
Dialogue: 0,0:01:58.39,0:01:59.59,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Cut it in half.
Dialogue: 0,0:01:59.67,0:02:03.35,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}You get two magnets, each\Nwith a north and a south.
Dialogue: 0,0:02:03.83,0:02:07.04,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}No one has ever found\Na lone magnetic pole.
Dialogue: 0,0:02:07.78,0:02:11.57,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}{\c&H3468EB&}Field lines{\c&HF7F4F2&} say the same:\Nthey never start or stop.
Dialogue: 0,0:02:12.07,0:02:14.20,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Draw any box, and count.
Dialogue: 0,0:02:14.39,0:02:16.80,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}As many lines go in as come out.
Dialogue: 0,0:02:17.18,0:02:18.92,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Keep this box in mind.
Dialogue: 0,0:02:19.97,0:02:25.01,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}A bicycle dynamo turns motion into\Nelectricity, using a built-in magnet.
Dialogue: 0,0:02:25.42,0:02:29.47,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}The Earth has to make its\Nown. The trick: a loop.
Dialogue: 0,0:02:30.21,0:02:33.88,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Hold a microphone near its own\Nspeaker, and turn up the {\c&H7AAF1B&}gain{\c&HF7F4F2&}.
Dialogue: 0,0:02:34.32,0:02:37.33,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Below a certain point, a whisper fades.
Dialogue: 0,0:02:37.64,0:02:40.41,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Past it, the loop feeds itself.
Dialogue: 0,0:02:43.15,0:02:46.76,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Now spin a copper disc wired\Nto a coil around its axle.
Dialogue: 0,0:02:47.00,0:02:49.92,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Current makes {\c&H3468EB&}field{\c&HF7F4F2&},\Nand field makes current.
Dialogue: 0,0:02:50.26,0:02:54.29,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Spin it faster and faster.\NDoes the field grow bit by bit,
Dialogue: 0,0:02:54.37,0:02:56.89,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}or not at all until some speed?
Dialogue: 0,0:03:00.63,0:03:03.03,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Not at all, until a threshold.
Dialogue: 0,0:03:03.47,0:03:06.23,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Past it, each turn multiplies a tiny
Dialogue: 0,0:03:06.31,0:03:10.58,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}{\c&H3468EB&}seed field{\c&HF7F4F2&} by the same factor:\Nexponential growth.
Dialogue: 0,0:03:11.11,0:03:14.18,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Just past the threshold, growth is slow.
Dialogue: 0,0:03:14.45,0:03:18.27,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Then the field brakes the\Ndisc, and growth stops.
Dialogue: 0,0:03:19.50,0:03:21.06,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Who pays for all this?
Dialogue: 0,0:03:21.46,0:03:25.87,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}A motor drives the {\c&HE58739&}flow{\c&HF7F4F2&};\Nin the Earth, convection does.
Dialogue: 0,0:03:26.31,0:03:30.35,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}The flow stretches the field;\Nthe field brakes the flow.
Dialogue: 0,0:03:30.81,0:03:36.32,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Once growth stops, every watt that\Ngoes in leaves as heat, on average.
Dialogue: 0,0:03:36.63,0:03:41.33,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}A dynamo turns motion into\Nfield; it creates no energy.
Dialogue: 0,0:03:42.37,0:03:46.84,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Earth's {\c&H3468EB&}field{\c&HF7F4F2&} points one way today.\NWhich way should it prefer?
Dialogue: 0,0:03:50.08,0:03:54.48,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Reverse every field line:\Nthe dynamo's equations read the same.
Dialogue: 0,0:03:54.79,0:04:00.19,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}So, neither. The two directions\Nare mirror twins, equally lawful.
Dialogue: 0,0:04:00.46,0:04:06.46,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Which one you get is history, not law:\Nin the disc, the seed's sign decides.
Dialogue: 0,0:04:06.74,0:04:09.33,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}And nothing in the laws forbids a flip.
Dialogue: 0,0:04:10.77,0:04:15.42,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Moving iron, a loop with a threshold,\Nno favourite direction.
Dialogue: 0,0:04:15.85,0:04:19.46,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}But the disc had wires;\Nliquid iron has none.
Dialogue: 0,0:04:19.95,0:04:23.04,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Then what motion can do it, and how fast?
Dialogue: 0,0:04:24.37,0:04:27.32,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Near the threshold,\Nshould dynamo simulations
Dialogue: 0,0:04:27.40,0:04:30.83,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}start from a tiny seed,\Na strong one, or both?
Dialogue: 0,0:04:36.27,0:04:39.04,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}What kind of motion can\Ngrow a {\c&H3468EB&}magnetic field{\c&HF7F4F2&}?
Dialogue: 0,0:04:40.02,0:04:45.03,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}In a perfect conductor, field lines\Nwould be threads frozen into the fluid.
Dialogue: 0,0:04:45.27,0:04:49.84,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Wherever the {\c&HE58739&}flow{\c&HF7F4F2&} stretches a thread,\Nthe field along it grows stronger.
Dialogue: 0,0:04:50.58,0:04:54.14,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Real metals let the threads\Nsmudge, like spreading ink.
Dialogue: 0,0:04:54.44,0:04:58.13,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}The smudging speed is set by the\Nmetal's {\c&H7AAF1B&}magnetic diffusivity{\c&HF7F4F2&}.
Dialogue: 0,0:04:58.37,0:05:00.56,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Same flow, two metals.
Dialogue: 0,0:05:00.82,0:05:04.70,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}A good conductor smudges slowly:\Nthe threads stay sharp.
Dialogue: 0,0:05:05.04,0:05:09.04,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}A poor one smudges fast:\Nthe threads blur away.
Dialogue: 0,0:05:10.07,0:05:15.06,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}One number keeps the score:\Nthe {\c&H7AAF1B&}magnetic Reynolds number{\c&HF7F4F2&}, Rm.
Dialogue: 0,0:05:15.44,0:05:21.03,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}It is the {\c&HE58739&}flow's speed{\c&HF7F4F2&}, times its size,\Ndivided by the magnetic diffusivity.
Dialogue: 0,0:05:21.57,0:05:25.30,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Large Rm: stretching outpaces smudging.
Dialogue: 0,0:05:25.69,0:05:28.64,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Small Rm: smudging wins.
Dialogue: 0,0:05:29.38,0:05:33.37,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Turn the Rm knob up,\Nand watch a weak seed field.
Dialogue: 0,0:05:33.87,0:05:36.84,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}At low Rm, the seed dies away.
Dialogue: 0,0:05:37.19,0:05:43.19,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Past a threshold, the growth rate crosses\Nzero and the seed grows, like the squeal.
Dialogue: 0,0:05:44.50,0:05:47.16,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Laboratory dynamos use liquid sodium.
Dialogue: 0,0:05:47.44,0:05:49.65,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}It flows as easily as water.
Dialogue: 0,0:05:49.96,0:05:54.87,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}But a field smudges in it about a hundred\Nthousand times faster than its swirls do.
Dialogue: 0,0:05:55.20,0:06:01.20,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Their ratio, one in a hundred thousand,\Nis the {\c&H7AAF1B&}magnetic Prandtl number{\c&HF7F4F2&}, Pm.
Dialogue: 0,0:06:02.06,0:06:05.93,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Reaching an Rm of a few\Ntens takes violent stirring.
Dialogue: 0,0:06:06.33,0:06:11.02,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}The flow's {\c&H7AAF1B&}Reynolds number{\c&HF7F4F2&}, the gauge\Nof turbulence, reaches millions.
Dialogue: 0,0:06:11.21,0:06:14.30,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}So every laboratory dynamo is turbulent.
Dialogue: 0,0:06:15.34,0:06:17.62,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Only three liquid-sodium experiments have
Dialogue: 0,0:06:17.70,0:06:20.56,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}made their own field,\Neach a different kind.
Dialogue: 0,0:06:21.04,0:06:26.30,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}In Riga, a corkscrew flow made a field\Nthat oscillates and slowly turns.
Dialogue: 0,0:06:26.54,0:06:32.54,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}In Karlsruhe, fifty-two spiralling cells\Nmade a steady field, close to a {\c&H3468EB&}dipole{\c&HF7F4F2&}.
Dialogue: 0,0:06:32.95,0:06:37.73,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}And only VKS, at Cadarache,\Nmade a dipole that can flip.
Dialogue: 0,0:06:38.47,0:06:42.07,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}A fourth, DRESDYN in Dresden,\Naims to join them:
Dialogue: 0,0:06:42.15,0:06:45.86,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}a spinning cylinder\Nwhose axis slowly turns.
Dialogue: 0,0:06:46.90,0:06:52.41,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Inside VKS: a cylinder holding a hundred\Nand fifty litres of liquid sodium.
Dialogue: 0,0:06:52.83,0:06:58.69,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}At its ends, two bladed discs, the\Nimpellers, spin in opposite directions.
Dialogue: 0,0:06:59.43,0:07:02.11,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Should it matter what metal\Nthe discs are made of?
Dialogue: 0,0:07:05.35,0:07:10.14,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}With stainless steel discs, there\Nwas no dynamo, even at full speed.
Dialogue: 0,0:07:10.44,0:07:15.47,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Swap in discs of soft iron,\Nand a dipole grows along the axis.
Dialogue: 0,0:07:17.21,0:07:21.80,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}So the threshold depends on more\Nthan Rm: materials count too.
Dialogue: 0,0:07:22.84,0:07:25.68,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Now spin the two discs\Nat different speeds.
Dialogue: 0,0:07:26.22,0:07:30.95,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}At some settings, the dipole flips\Nagain and again, with no schedule.
Dialogue: 0,0:07:31.28,0:07:34.95,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Like the compass needle at the\Nstart, but every minute or two.
Dialogue: 0,0:07:35.69,0:07:38.88,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Each flip itself takes\Nonly about five seconds.
Dialogue: 0,0:07:39.26,0:07:45.26,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Uneven stripes, like the seafloor's, but\Nwritten in minutes, not millions of years.
Dialogue: 0,0:07:46.40,0:07:49.21,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Simulations cannot reach sodium's tiny {\c&H7AAF1B&}Pm{\c&HF7F4F2&}:
Dialogue: 0,0:07:49.29,0:07:53.89,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}which Pm values should the first\Nthree-dimensional runs cover?
Dialogue: 0,0:07:59.34,0:08:04.36,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Riga, Karlsruhe and VKS crowned\Na hunt of nearly a century.
Dialogue: 0,0:08:04.71,0:08:05.92,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Here is its map.
Dialogue: 0,0:08:06.66,0:08:12.66,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Lane one, the Earth's record: lavas\Nmagnetized backwards, nineteen oh six.
Dialogue: 0,0:08:13.26,0:08:16.48,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Seafloor stripes, nineteen sixty-three.
Dialogue: 0,0:08:16.78,0:08:21.47,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Then flips as chance events,\Nwhose odds change over the ages.
Dialogue: 0,0:08:22.50,0:08:24.44,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Lane two, theory.
Dialogue: 0,0:08:24.83,0:08:30.49,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Nineteen nineteen: a moving, conducting\Nfluid might keep the Sun's field alive.
Dialogue: 0,0:08:30.93,0:08:36.91,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Nineteen thirty-three: no flow can sustain\Na field that is symmetric around an axis.
Dialogue: 0,0:08:37.65,0:08:42.29,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Nineteen fifty-eight: the first\Nproven examples of flows that can.
Dialogue: 0,0:08:42.58,0:08:45.32,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}In the seventies came simple dynamo flows,
Dialogue: 0,0:08:45.40,0:08:48.61,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}later the blueprints\Nof Riga and Karlsruhe.
Dialogue: 0,0:08:49.67,0:08:52.53,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Lane three: building a dynamo.
Dialogue: 0,0:08:52.78,0:08:57.74,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Nineteen sixty-three: a model\Ndynamo of spinning iron cylinders.
Dialogue: 0,0:08:57.96,0:09:03.96,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Nineteen ninety-nine: sodium makes its\Nown field, in Riga, then Karlsruhe.
Dialogue: 0,0:09:04.91,0:09:08.60,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Two thousand six: VKS,\Nonly with iron discs,
Dialogue: 0,0:09:08.68,0:09:13.30,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}then the first flips of a\Nturbulent sodium dynamo.
Dialogue: 0,0:09:14.74,0:09:16.88,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Lane four, computers.
Dialogue: 0,0:09:17.34,0:09:21.63,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Nineteen eighty-one: simulated\Nturbulence grows dynamos.
Dialogue: 0,0:09:22.13,0:09:27.07,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Nineteen ninety-five: a simulated\NEarth's core reverses its field.
Dialogue: 0,0:09:27.48,0:09:31.92,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Ringed pins mark papers co-signed\Nby this project's supervisors.
Dialogue: 0,0:09:32.57,0:09:38.02,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Nineteen ninety-seven: two simulated\Ncounter-rotating eddies make a dynamo.
Dialogue: 0,0:09:38.38,0:09:43.63,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Two thousand five: such dynamos\Nstill work at a {\c&H7AAF1B&}Pm{\c&HF7F4F2&} of one hundredth.
Dialogue: 0,0:09:44.37,0:09:50.37,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}From twenty sixteen, two codes simulate\Nthe VKS tank, iron impellers included.
Dialogue: 0,0:09:51.17,0:09:53.61,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Twenty twenty-six: both make it flip,
Dialogue: 0,0:09:53.69,0:09:57.78,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}in the runs explored only\Nwith conducting surroundings.
Dialogue: 0,0:09:58.03,0:10:03.47,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Their reference run: a {\c&H7AAF1B&}Reynolds number{\c&HF7F4F2&}\Nnear sixteen hundred, not millions.
Dialogue: 0,0:10:04.50,0:10:07.83,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Lane five: a few dials instead of a tank.
Dialogue: 0,0:10:08.39,0:10:12.89,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Nineteen fifty-eight: two coupled\Ndisc dynamos already flip.
Dialogue: 0,0:10:13.38,0:10:19.38,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Twenty twelve: three dials that flip by\Nchaos alone; we will play with them soon.
Dialogue: 0,0:10:20.41,0:10:25.06,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Twenty twenty-six: four dials\Nfitted to the simulated VKS flips,
Dialogue: 0,0:10:25.14,0:10:28.50,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}by a method that finds equations in data.
Dialogue: 0,0:10:29.54,0:10:31.57,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}MilaDy began in twenty twenty-six,
Dialogue: 0,0:10:31.65,0:10:35.95,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}bringing machine learning where\Ntanks, computers and dials meet.
Dialogue: 0,0:10:36.54,0:10:40.33,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Yet the data-driven lane is\Nthin: in twenty twenty-two,
Dialogue: 0,0:10:40.41,0:10:46.41,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}the learning methods one study tried could\Nnot reliably foresee a geomagnetic flip.
Dialogue: 0,0:10:47.33,0:10:50.44,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}The internship and a\Nproposed thesis add a bet:
Dialogue: 0,0:10:50.52,0:10:54.94,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}{\c&HF09CB4&}machines{\c&HF7F4F2&} that dream up dynamo\Nfields and obey their physics.
Dialogue: 0,0:10:55.68,0:11:00.49,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Behind every lane, the same race:\Nstretching against smudging.
Dialogue: 0,0:11:04.74,0:11:09.79,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Back to the race: the {\c&HE58739&}flow{\c&HF7F4F2&} stretches\Nthe field, and diffusion smudges it.
Dialogue: 0,0:11:10.15,0:11:13.99,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Physicists write the very same\Nrace as a single equation.
Dialogue: 0,0:11:14.73,0:11:19.40,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}On the left: how fast the\N{\c&H3468EB&}magnetic field{\c&HF7F4F2&} changes, at each point.
Dialogue: 0,0:11:20.10,0:11:22.75,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}First term on the right:\Nthe flow carries the
Dialogue: 0,0:11:22.83,0:11:26.01,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}field along and stretches\Nit, like the threads.
Dialogue: 0,0:11:26.54,0:11:32.33,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Second term: diffusion smudges it,\Nat a pace set by the magnetic diffusivity.
Dialogue: 0,0:11:32.81,0:11:35.50,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}A poor conductor smudges fast.
Dialogue: 0,0:11:35.84,0:11:40.21,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Stretching against smudging:\Nthe whole race, in one line.
Dialogue: 0,0:11:41.24,0:11:45.20,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Picture the {\c&HE58739&}flow{\c&HF7F4F2&} as a conveyor\Nbelt that does not feel its cargo.
Dialogue: 0,0:11:45.65,0:11:51.28,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}A weak {\c&H3468EB&}field{\c&HF7F4F2&} rides on the imposed flow,\Nand fades or grows exponentially.
Dialogue: 0,0:11:51.71,0:11:56.29,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}On a log scale: a straight line,\Nwhose slope is the growth rate.
Dialogue: 0,0:11:57.03,0:11:59.78,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}But a strong field\Npushes back on the flow,
Dialogue: 0,0:11:59.86,0:12:04.14,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}as it braked the copper disc:\Nthe line bends into a plateau.
Dialogue: 0,0:12:04.48,0:12:07.48,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}These are the kinematic\Nand dynamic regimes.
Dialogue: 0,0:12:07.70,0:12:09.89,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Flips live on the plateau.
Dialogue: 0,0:12:10.94,0:12:14.32,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}To test ideas, physicists\Nneed a {\c&HE58739&}flow{\c&HF7F4F2&} simple enough
Dialogue: 0,0:12:14.40,0:12:18.00,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}to write on one line,\Nyet able to make a dynamo.
Dialogue: 0,0:12:18.40,0:12:21.79,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}One classic choice: the ABC flow.
Dialogue: 0,0:12:22.53,0:12:25.57,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}It fills space with\Nidentical twisting rooms.
Dialogue: 0,0:12:25.90,0:12:28.76,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Three crossed winds make\Nthe fluid corkscrew:
Dialogue: 0,0:12:28.84,0:12:31.72,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}it spins around the direction it moves.
Dialogue: 0,0:12:32.08,0:12:34.79,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}This box returns higher on our climb.
Dialogue: 0,0:12:35.84,0:12:41.84,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Now turn one {\c&H7AAF1B&}knob{\c&HF7F4F2&} on this flow: Rm.\NTurning it up means less diffusion.
Dialogue: 0,0:12:42.46,0:12:45.11,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Less smudging should\Nonly help the stretching.
Dialogue: 0,0:12:45.85,0:12:49.89,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}So once the dynamo switches\Non, it stays on. Right?
Dialogue: 0,0:12:53.63,0:12:56.83,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Here is the growth rate,\Ncomputed at twenty-four settings.
Dialogue: 0,0:12:57.24,0:13:03.24,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Below about nine, every {\c&H3468EB&}field{\c&HF7F4F2&} fades. Then\Ngrowth turns positive: the dynamo is on.
Dialogue: 0,0:13:04.47,0:13:07.46,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Near seventeen and a half,\Nit switches off again.
Dialogue: 0,0:13:07.79,0:13:10.45,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Above about twenty-seven, it comes back.
Dialogue: 0,0:13:10.53,0:13:15.95,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Not one threshold, but windows: turning\Na knob further does not always help.
Dialogue: 0,0:13:16.20,0:13:19.20,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}It agrees with published\Nresults. No machine
Dialogue: 0,0:13:19.28,0:13:22.49,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}learning yet: this only tests the solver.
Dialogue: 0,0:13:23.74,0:13:27.17,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Real dynamos have not\None {\c&H7AAF1B&}knob{\c&HF7F4F2&}, but a panel.
Dialogue: 0,0:13:27.48,0:13:30.93,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}How hard the metal is stirred;\Nhow well it conducts.
Dialogue: 0,0:13:31.11,0:13:34.13,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}What the discs are made\Nof: remember the iron.
Dialogue: 0,0:13:34.37,0:13:39.09,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}How unequal their speeds are:\Nthat is when VKS flipped.
Dialogue: 0,0:13:39.39,0:13:44.50,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}For DRESDYN, a precessing tank:\Nhow fast its axis turns.
Dialogue: 0,0:13:45.24,0:13:49.21,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Even the wall counts: in VKS\Nsimulations, changing only its
Dialogue: 0,0:13:49.29,0:13:53.96,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}magnetic condition raised the\Nthreshold by about forty percent.
Dialogue: 0,0:13:55.00,0:13:59.68,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Every turbulent dynamo simulation\Nin three dimensions is very costly.
Dialogue: 0,0:13:59.85,0:14:02.93,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}So only a handful of knob\Nsettings get simulated.
Dialogue: 0,0:14:03.40,0:14:09.32,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Each one is a mountain of data: a single\Nsnapshot can weigh more than a gigabyte.
Dialogue: 0,0:14:10.06,0:14:12.56,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Each run holds only a few flips.
Dialogue: 0,0:14:12.70,0:14:15.61,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}And between the dots,\Nthere is nothing at all.
Dialogue: 0,0:14:16.67,0:14:19.14,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Those gaps are where this film lives.
Dialogue: 0,0:14:19.75,0:14:22.71,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}The study behind this\Nfilm asks three things.
Dialogue: 0,0:14:23.45,0:14:26.92,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}One: can a {\c&HF09CB4&}machine{\c&HF7F4F2&} learn\Nthe whole family of fields,
Dialogue: 0,0:14:27.00,0:14:30.00,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}even at knob settings between the dots?
Dialogue: 0,0:14:30.72,0:14:34.04,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Two: can it respect\Neach law where it holds,
Dialogue: 0,0:14:34.12,0:14:38.70,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}exactly in every field,\Nor {\c&H00A1ED&}on average{\c&HF7F4F2&} over many fields?
Dialogue: 0,0:14:39.32,0:14:43.13,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Three: can it tell us\N{\c&H00A1ED&}how often{\c&HF7F4F2&} the field flips?
Dialogue: 0,0:14:43.87,0:14:47.01,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}To find out, the study's\Nplan climbs a staircase,
Dialogue: 0,0:14:47.09,0:14:50.91,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}from three numbers to the\Nshapes of real experiments.
Dialogue: 0,0:14:51.65,0:14:55.36,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}One honest note: this\Nindependent study is prospective,
Dialogue: 0,0:14:55.44,0:14:58.88,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}built on small experiments\Non an ordinary computer,
Dialogue: 0,0:14:58.96,0:15:03.43,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}and not endorsed by MilaDy,\Nits partners or the supervisors.
Dialogue: 0,0:15:05.07,0:15:10.08,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}An equation, windows, a panel\Nof knobs, a handful of dots.
Dialogue: 0,0:15:10.63,0:15:12.51,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Full simulations cost too much,
Dialogue: 0,0:15:12.59,0:15:16.72,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}so we start with a three-dial\Ncaricature that already flips.
Dialogue: 0,0:15:17.04,0:15:19.27,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Can anyone say when it flips next?
Dialogue: 0,0:15:20.60,0:15:23.39,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}With only a handful of runs,\Nshould the {\c&H7AAF1B&}settings{\c&HF7F4F2&}
Dialogue: 0,0:15:23.47,0:15:26.67,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}be spread evenly,\Nor packed near each threshold?
Dialogue: 0,0:15:31.90,0:15:36.18,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Here is the caricature:\Nthree dials, linked by springs.
Dialogue: 0,0:15:36.50,0:15:39.80,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}One is the {\c&H3468EB&}dipole{\c&HF7F4F2&},\Nthe north-south part of the field.
Dialogue: 0,0:15:40.27,0:15:43.46,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}One is the {\c&H3468EB&}quadrupole{\c&HF7F4F2&}, a four-pole part.
Dialogue: 0,0:15:43.87,0:15:47.93,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}The third is a {\c&HE58739&}flow{\c&HF7F4F2&} pattern,\Ndriven by a knob, Gamma.
Dialogue: 0,0:15:48.67,0:15:52.69,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Three short equations, from\NGissinger, tie them together.
Dialogue: 0,0:15:53.14,0:15:56.81,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Flip both field dials,\Nand the equations read the same.
Dialogue: 0,0:15:57.23,0:16:01.83,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Three dials, three numbers:\Nthe bottom step of the staircase.
Dialogue: 0,0:16:02.87,0:16:07.95,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Use two dials as a map:\N{\c&H3468EB&}quadrupole{\c&HF7F4F2&} across, dipole up.
Dialogue: 0,0:16:08.57,0:16:13.27,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}With the knob low, the state\Ncircles in one whirlpool, and stays.
Dialogue: 0,0:16:13.69,0:16:17.92,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}A mirror twin circles the\Nmirror whirlpool: dipole down.
Dialogue: 0,0:16:18.31,0:16:22.70,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Each start is trapped.\NThe dipole never changes sign.
Dialogue: 0,0:16:23.44,0:16:24.86,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Now turn {\c&H7AAF1B&}Gamma{\c&HF7F4F2&} up.
Dialogue: 0,0:16:25.22,0:16:31.22,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Just above zero point eight six, the\Nwhirlpools touch: a narrow corridor opens.
Dialogue: 0,0:16:31.94,0:16:37.57,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}The state lingers on one side,\Nthen slips through. The {\c&H3468EB&}dipole{\c&HF7F4F2&} flips.
Dialogue: 0,0:16:37.82,0:16:42.60,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Nothing here is random. So,\Nwill the flips come like clockwork?
Dialogue: 0,0:16:45.84,0:16:49.46,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}No. Long waits and\Nshort ones, irregularly.
Dialogue: 0,0:16:49.77,0:16:53.68,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}The dipole's sign prints uneven\Nstripes, like the seafloor.
Dialogue: 0,0:16:54.70,0:16:59.30,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Now count: one tick each\Ntime the {\c&H3468EB&}dipole{\c&HF7F4F2&} crosses zero.
Dialogue: 0,0:16:59.75,0:17:04.59,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Not every tick is a flip: an excursion\Ncan cross and come straight back.
Dialogue: 0,0:17:05.01,0:17:08.17,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Ticks per unit of time: the {\c&H00A1ED&}crossing rate{\c&HF7F4F2&}.
Dialogue: 0,0:17:08.91,0:17:12.12,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Up to zero point eight six: silence.
Dialogue: 0,0:17:12.52,0:17:16.22,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Just above, about two ticks\Nper thousand time units.
Dialogue: 0,0:17:16.65,0:17:19.63,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}At {\c&H7AAF1B&}Gamma{\c&HF7F4F2&} equal to one, about eleven.
Dialogue: 0,0:17:20.67,0:17:26.17,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Set {\c&H7AAF1B&}Gamma{\c&HF7F4F2&} at zero point nine, and\Nlaunch two copies, one millionth apart.
Dialogue: 0,0:17:26.53,0:17:31.60,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}We know the state to six decimals.\NCan we predict the date of the next flip?
Dialogue: 0,0:17:35.34,0:17:38.01,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}At first, the copies move as one.
Dialogue: 0,0:17:38.22,0:17:41.72,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}But their gap nearly triples\Nevery eleven time units.
Dialogue: 0,0:17:42.17,0:17:45.87,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}After about a hundred and fifty,\Nthey disagree completely.
Dialogue: 0,0:17:46.61,0:17:50.84,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}The average wait between crossings\Nis longer: over two hundred.
Dialogue: 0,0:17:51.31,0:17:55.41,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Each extra decimal buys only\Nabout twenty-five more time units.
Dialogue: 0,0:17:55.55,0:18:01.34,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}So the forecast fades before the average\Nwait ends. Only the statistics survive.
Dialogue: 0,0:18:02.37,0:18:05.52,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}The path is lost,\Nso leave the shutter open.
Dialogue: 0,0:18:06.00,0:18:09.82,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Each pass leaves a little\Nlight: a glow builds.
Dialogue: 0,0:18:10.12,0:18:14.07,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}A copy started on the other side\Nfirst paints the mirror image.
Dialogue: 0,0:18:14.51,0:18:17.84,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Given time, both paint the same glow.
Dialogue: 0,0:18:18.58,0:18:23.45,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Where does the {\c&H3468EB&}dipole{\c&HF7F4F2&} spend its\Ntime? Two humps, the same for both.
Dialogue: 0,0:18:23.99,0:18:27.49,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}This reproducible glow is\Nthe stationary distribution.
Dialogue: 0,0:18:27.98,0:18:32.02,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Below the threshold, each\Nstart paints its own whirlpool.
Dialogue: 0,0:18:33.07,0:18:37.39,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Now film the trajectory,\None frame every two time units.
Dialogue: 0,0:18:37.84,0:18:39.77,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Neighbouring frames look alike.
Dialogue: 0,0:18:40.35,0:18:42.18,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Cut the film into frames.
Dialogue: 0,0:18:42.36,0:18:46.97,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Drop them in a shoebox, shake,\Nand throw away the timestamps.
Dialogue: 0,0:18:47.39,0:18:51.75,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Pour the box out:\Nthe frames redraw the same glow.
Dialogue: 0,0:18:52.49,0:18:56.75,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Each frame is a snapshot:\Nthree dial readings, nothing more.
Dialogue: 0,0:18:57.05,0:19:00.07,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}It carries no timestamp and no time order.
Dialogue: 0,0:19:00.36,0:19:04.43,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Yet the shoebox, plus the equations,\Nanswers many questions.
Dialogue: 0,0:19:04.77,0:19:09.75,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Averages, energy budgets, even\Nhow often the dipole crosses zero.
Dialogue: 0,0:19:10.80,0:19:14.74,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Pick one photo. Its three\Ndial readings give its energy.
Dialogue: 0,0:19:15.03,0:19:19.78,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}From that photo alone, the equations\Ntell how fast its energy changes.
Dialogue: 0,0:19:20.25,0:19:23.26,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Drop one marble per photo at that value.
Dialogue: 0,0:19:24.00,0:19:29.07,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Some photos are gaining energy,\Nothers losing it. The pile spreads wide.
Dialogue: 0,0:19:29.29,0:19:31.48,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Yet its average sits at zero.
Dialogue: 0,0:19:31.82,0:19:35.11,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Energy in equals energy out, on average.
Dialogue: 0,0:19:35.85,0:19:38.33,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Like a bank account: over the years,
Dialogue: 0,0:19:38.41,0:19:42.51,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}income matches spending,\Nyet each day gains or loses.
Dialogue: 0,0:19:42.86,0:19:48.27,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}This is a {\c&H00A1ED&}law of the crowd{\c&HF7F4F2&}. It holds\Nfor the shoebox, not for each photo.
Dialogue: 0,0:19:49.40,0:19:52.56,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}How many independent snapshots\Ndoes each setting need,
Dialogue: 0,0:19:52.64,0:19:55.18,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}and how far apart must they be saved?
Dialogue: 0,0:20:00.44,0:20:04.58,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}A puzzle. How many cars\Ncross this line each hour?
Dialogue: 0,0:20:04.82,0:20:10.21,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}No clock. Just one photo from above,\Nwhere every speedometer can be read.
Dialogue: 0,0:20:10.95,0:20:14.66,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Can one photo, with no time\Nin it, give you a rate?
Dialogue: 0,0:20:17.90,0:20:21.95,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}It can. Draw a thin strip around the line.
Dialogue: 0,0:20:22.45,0:20:27.67,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}A fast car spends little time inside,\Nso catching one there counts for more.
Dialogue: 0,0:20:28.41,0:20:32.86,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Add up the speeds of the cars inside,\Nand divide by the strip's width.
Dialogue: 0,0:20:33.27,0:20:37.03,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}On average, that is how\Nmany cars cross per hour.
Dialogue: 0,0:20:38.07,0:20:42.43,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Back to the shoebox. Spread its\Nphotos out by their {\c&H3468EB&}dipole{\c&HF7F4F2&} reading.
Dialogue: 0,0:20:42.60,0:20:46.85,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}The line to cross is zero:\Ndraw a thin strip around it.
Dialogue: 0,0:20:47.59,0:20:50.09,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Each photo also needs a speedometer.
Dialogue: 0,0:20:50.49,0:20:53.31,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}No clock required:\Nthe dipole's equation turns
Dialogue: 0,0:20:53.39,0:20:56.56,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}the photo's three dial\Nreadings into a speed.
Dialogue: 0,0:20:56.97,0:21:01.34,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Up or down, both count,\Nso keep only its size.
Dialogue: 0,0:21:02.08,0:21:03.87,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Add the speeds inside the strip,
Dialogue: 0,0:21:03.95,0:21:07.73,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}then divide by its width and\Nby the total number of photos.
Dialogue: 0,0:21:08.05,0:21:09.85,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Out comes the {\c&H00A1ED&}crossing rate{\c&HF7F4F2&}.
Dialogue: 0,0:21:10.33,0:21:13.59,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Shrink the strip to nothing,\Nand for well-behaved
Dialogue: 0,0:21:13.67,0:21:16.99,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}systems the recipe is\Nexact: Rice's formula.
Dialogue: 0,0:21:17.44,0:21:21.22,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}The catch: few photos land\Nin a thin strip. Here,
Dialogue: 0,0:21:21.30,0:21:24.62,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}about two dozen out of eight thousand.
Dialogue: 0,0:21:25.67,0:21:27.54,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Now test it against the truth.
Dialogue: 0,0:21:27.94,0:21:29.75,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}At forty-one settings of {\c&H7AAF1B&}Gamma{\c&HF7F4F2&},
Dialogue: 0,0:21:29.83,0:21:33.33,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}long simulations count the\Ncrossings as they happen.
Dialogue: 0,0:21:33.60,0:21:38.80,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Rice gets only the true snapshots,\Nshuffled, plus the equations.
Dialogue: 0,0:21:39.54,0:21:41.91,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}The two curves almost coincide.
Dialogue: 0,0:21:42.37,0:21:45.00,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Shade the gap,\Nand divide by the area under
Dialogue: 0,0:21:45.08,0:21:47.93,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}the counted curve: the {\c&H00A1ED&}cumulative error{\c&HF7F4F2&}.
Dialogue: 0,0:21:48.32,0:21:51.85,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Four percent near the threshold,\Nsix percent above it.
Dialogue: 0,0:21:52.27,0:21:54.72,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}A good sign, on three chaotic dials that
Dialogue: 0,0:21:54.80,0:21:57.86,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}are not even guaranteed\Nto be well-behaved.
Dialogue: 0,0:21:59.30,0:22:03.63,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}One caution: Rice counts\Ncrossings, not reversals.
Dialogue: 0,0:22:03.98,0:22:09.04,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}An excursion across zero and back\Nadds two crossings, but no reversal.
Dialogue: 0,0:22:09.49,0:22:12.20,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}To isolate reversals, the PhD plan counts
Dialogue: 0,0:22:12.28,0:22:15.60,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}crossings of a far level,\Non the other side.
Dialogue: 0,0:22:16.84,0:22:19.98,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}So Rice needs only\Nsnapshots and the equations,
Dialogue: 0,0:22:20.06,0:22:23.14,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}but plenty of snapshots, and cheap ones.
Dialogue: 0,0:22:23.58,0:22:28.05,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Yet real dynamo simulations exist\Nat only a few {\c&H7AAF1B&}knob settings{\c&HF7F4F2&}.
Dialogue: 0,0:22:28.49,0:22:31.87,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Between the dots,\Nthere is no shoebox at all.
Dialogue: 0,0:22:32.33,0:22:35.83,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}How do you get snapshots at\Na setting nobody simulated?
Dialogue: 0,0:22:37.37,0:22:40.03,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}For VKS, which dipole should Rice count,
Dialogue: 0,0:22:40.11,0:22:44.00,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}and what threshold turns a\Ncrossing into a reversal?
Dialogue: 0,0:22:49.24,0:22:54.56,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}New snapshots start as pure noise:\Na round cloud of random points.
Dialogue: 0,0:22:55.00,0:23:00.08,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Over it, the crowd we want:\None point per snapshot. Here,
Dialogue: 0,0:23:00.16,0:23:04.29,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}two mirror blobs, like the two whirlpools.
Dialogue: 0,0:23:05.03,0:23:07.51,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Now let a {\c&HF09CB4&}wind{\c&HF7F4F2&} blow through the cloud.
Dialogue: 0,0:23:07.83,0:23:12.85,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Each point drifts, bends,\Nand lands inside the shape of the data.
Dialogue: 0,0:23:13.15,0:23:17.92,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Fresh noise in, fresh snapshots\Nout, as many as you like.
Dialogue: 0,0:23:18.66,0:23:24.10,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}That wind is a {\c&HF09CB4&}generative model{\c&HF7F4F2&}:\Nnew, plausible examples, on demand.
Dialogue: 0,0:23:24.53,0:23:26.97,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}The whole game is learning the wind.
Dialogue: 0,0:23:28.00,0:23:29.96,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}One warning before the formulas.
Dialogue: 0,0:23:30.32,0:23:34.69,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}The wind runs on its own\N{\c&HF09CB4&}transport clock{\c&HF7F4F2&}, from zero to one.
Dialogue: 0,0:23:34.98,0:23:40.64,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Not physical time: at the end,\Nyou hold one snapshot, not a movie.
Dialogue: 0,0:23:41.38,0:23:47.16,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}And the {\c&HF09CB4&}wind's arrows{\c&HF7F4F2&} move snapshots, not\Nfluid: they are not the fluid's velocity.
Dialogue: 0,0:23:48.20,0:23:51.43,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}How is the wind learned?\NWith a simple trick.
Dialogue: 0,0:23:51.90,0:23:56.83,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Keep one coordinate of each point, and\Nlay the transport clock out to the right.
Dialogue: 0,0:23:57.30,0:24:00.18,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Pair each noise point\Nwith a random snapshot;
Dialogue: 0,0:24:00.26,0:24:02.76,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}join them with a straight string.
Dialogue: 0,0:24:03.01,0:24:06.05,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Thousands of strings, crossing everywhere.
Dialogue: 0,0:24:06.79,0:24:09.28,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Pick one place, at one moment.
Dialogue: 0,0:24:10.05,0:24:13.56,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Several strings pass through,\Npointing different ways.
Dialogue: 0,0:24:13.96,0:24:18.21,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}The network learns one arrow\Nthere: their {\c&HF09CB4&}average direction{\c&HF7F4F2&}.
Dialogue: 0,0:24:18.65,0:24:21.52,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}That is the whole training:\Nplain regression,
Dialogue: 0,0:24:21.60,0:24:24.29,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}like fitting a curve through points.
Dialogue: 0,0:24:25.03,0:24:28.17,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Follow the averaged wind, not the strings.
Dialogue: 0,0:24:28.42,0:24:32.76,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}The paths bend, and drawn against\Nthe clock, they never cross.
Dialogue: 0,0:24:33.15,0:24:38.25,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Learn the average exactly, and the\Nnoise lands exactly on the data's crowd.
Dialogue: 0,0:24:39.30,0:24:41.10,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Now give the wind a {\c&H7AAF1B&}knob{\c&HF7F4F2&}.
Dialogue: 0,0:24:41.29,0:24:45.39,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Turn it: the target changes\Nshape, and the {\c&HF09CB4&}cloud{\c&HF7F4F2&} follows.
Dialogue: 0,0:24:45.58,0:24:47.94,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}One network, every setting.
Dialogue: 0,0:24:48.68,0:24:52.94,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}On the three dials, a real network\Ntrained at the filled dots.
Dialogue: 0,0:24:53.20,0:24:56.95,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}It was tested at the open dots,\Nnever seen in training.
Dialogue: 0,0:24:57.25,0:25:02.59,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}In between, no theorem promises\Nsuccess: only tests can tell.
Dialogue: 0,0:25:03.00,0:25:05.79,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Beyond the range, nothing was tested.
Dialogue: 0,0:25:06.53,0:25:12.20,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}At an open dot, the network's {\c&HF09CB4&}snapshots{\c&HF7F4F2&}\Njoin the shoebox, beside simulated ones.
Dialogue: 0,0:25:13.24,0:25:18.13,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}A generated snapshot has no\Ntrue twin, so we compare crowds.
Dialogue: 0,0:25:18.55,0:25:21.16,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Picture each crowd as a pile of sand.
Dialogue: 0,0:25:21.47,0:25:25.31,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}A {\c&H00A1ED&}distribution distance{\c&HF7F4F2&} scores\Nhow different the two piles are.
Dialogue: 0,0:25:25.68,0:25:30.38,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}The earth-mover kind counts how\Nmuch sand must move, and how far.
Dialogue: 0,0:25:31.12,0:25:35.48,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}A perfect model, scored against\Nthe data: will it get zero?
Dialogue: 0,0:25:38.72,0:25:43.83,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}No. Roll a fair die a thousand\Ntimes, then a thousand more.
Dialogue: 0,0:25:44.07,0:25:48.52,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Same die, same truth,\Nyet the two histograms differ.
Dialogue: 0,0:25:49.01,0:25:53.70,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}That gap is the {\c&H00A1ED&}noise floor{\c&HF7F4F2&},\Nabout what a perfect model scores.
Dialogue: 0,0:25:54.10,0:25:59.74,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Judge every model against it,\Nat equal sample size, never against zero.
Dialogue: 0,0:26:00.48,0:26:03.94,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}One catch: the samples\Nmust be independent.
Dialogue: 0,0:26:04.30,0:26:08.79,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Overlap them, and the floor drops\Ntoo low, as the report warns.
Dialogue: 0,0:26:09.94,0:26:12.78,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Which statistics must the\Ngenerated fields match,
Dialogue: 0,0:26:12.86,0:26:16.23,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}and how close to the noise\Nfloor counts as success?
Dialogue: 0,0:26:21.27,0:26:24.22,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Physics hands the machine\Ntwo kinds of laws.
Dialogue: 0,0:26:24.42,0:26:26.98,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Some hold for every single snapshot.
Dialogue: 0,0:26:27.31,0:26:29.42,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Others hold only for the crowd.
Dialogue: 0,0:26:29.88,0:26:32.10,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Two shelves, still empty.
Dialogue: 0,0:26:32.84,0:26:37.00,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Back to the three dials, at a\N{\c&H7AAF1B&}Gamma{\c&HF7F4F2&} never seen in training.
Dialogue: 0,0:26:37.42,0:26:39.70,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Grey: true snapshots.
Dialogue: 0,0:26:40.03,0:26:42.57,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Violet: a plain {\c&HF09CB4&}generator{\c&HF7F4F2&}.
Dialogue: 0,0:26:42.84,0:26:44.23,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}The shape is right.
Dialogue: 0,0:26:44.97,0:26:46.54,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Now weigh the sides.
Dialogue: 0,0:26:47.06,0:26:50.09,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}The truth is balanced; the machine is not.
Dialogue: 0,0:26:50.57,0:26:53.62,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Only about four in ten of\Nits snapshots point up.
Dialogue: 0,0:26:54.14,0:26:56.98,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}It copied its data: below the threshold,
Dialogue: 0,0:26:57.06,0:27:01.84,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}each run stays in one whirlpool,\Nand the runs split unevenly.
Dialogue: 0,0:27:02.87,0:27:04.86,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Two ways to teach the mirror.
Dialogue: 0,0:27:05.19,0:27:07.52,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}The first student, data augmentation,
Dialogue: 0,0:27:07.60,0:27:11.18,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}sees each training snapshot\Nflipped half the time.
Dialogue: 0,0:27:11.54,0:27:15.45,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}The second, equivariance,\Nhas the mirror built into its wind:
Dialogue: 0,0:27:15.53,0:27:19.84,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}a mirrored noise point always\Nlands on the mirrored snapshot.
Dialogue: 0,0:27:20.58,0:27:24.15,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}At a {\c&H7AAF1B&}Gamma{\c&HF7F4F2&} neither has seen,\Nwho stays balanced?
Dialogue: 0,0:27:27.89,0:27:29.30,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}The built-in mirror.
Dialogue: 0,0:27:29.71,0:27:31.64,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}The first student tilts a little.
Dialogue: 0,0:27:31.83,0:27:36.30,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Yet in the mirror's crowd,\Neach snapshot still points one way.
Dialogue: 0,0:27:36.67,0:27:40.22,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}The mirror balances the\Ncrowd, not each snapshot.
Dialogue: 0,0:27:40.52,0:27:45.06,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Its card goes on the crowd\Nshelf: built in, exactly.
Dialogue: 0,0:27:46.10,0:27:48.74,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Here is that tilt, setting by setting.
Dialogue: 0,0:27:49.14,0:27:52.59,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Below the threshold,\Nruns often split unevenly:
Dialogue: 0,0:27:52.67,0:27:57.49,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}at zero point eight six, only\Nfive of sixteen runs point up.
Dialogue: 0,0:27:58.04,0:28:03.19,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}The plain {\c&HF09CB4&}machine{\c&HF7F4F2&} copies the imbalance,\Nand spills it onto unseen settings.
Dialogue: 0,0:28:03.93,0:28:07.44,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Augmentation sits on the {\c&H00A1ED&}noise floor{\c&HF7F4F2&}\Nat its training settings.
Dialogue: 0,0:28:07.71,0:28:11.93,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Between them, it drifts:\Nthe network fills the gaps freely.
Dialogue: 0,0:28:12.34,0:28:15.26,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}The built-in mirror stays\Non the floor everywhere.
Dialogue: 0,0:28:15.58,0:28:19.06,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Flipped copies teach the mirror\Nonly where there are data.
Dialogue: 0,0:28:20.10,0:28:22.28,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Can the energy budget catch the tilt?
Dialogue: 0,0:28:22.73,0:28:26.34,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}No. Each whirlpool\Nbalances its own budget.
Dialogue: 0,0:28:26.52,0:28:29.85,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}So a mix of the two passes,\Nin any proportion.
Dialogue: 0,0:28:30.26,0:28:35.18,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}On a check of crowd laws, the most\Nlopsided machine even edges ahead.
Dialogue: 0,0:28:35.53,0:28:37.75,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}The mirror needs its own tool.
Dialogue: 0,0:28:38.80,0:28:40.90,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Remember the budget of the shoebox.
Dialogue: 0,0:28:41.08,0:28:45.18,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Snapshot by snapshot,\Nenergy rises or falls.
Dialogue: 0,0:28:45.48,0:28:50.77,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Only the crowd balances, on average,\Nlike a bank account over the years.
Dialogue: 0,0:28:51.51,0:28:54.42,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Now, a tempting fix for the machine.
Dialogue: 0,0:28:54.64,0:29:00.64,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Force every generated snapshot to balance\Nits budget exactly. Better, or worse?
Dialogue: 0,0:29:05.11,0:29:06.18,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Worse.
Dialogue: 0,0:29:06.26,0:29:10.06,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Push each snapshot until\Nits energy stops changing:
Dialogue: 0,0:29:10.14,0:29:13.88,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}the wide pile collapses\Ninto a spike at zero.
Dialogue: 0,0:29:14.12,0:29:17.39,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}A ledger where every single day balances.
Dialogue: 0,0:29:17.68,0:29:19.88,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}No real account looks like that.
Dialogue: 0,0:29:20.62,0:29:24.20,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}The {\c&H00A1ED&}distribution distance{\c&HF7F4F2&}\Nto the truth grows ninefold.
Dialogue: 0,0:29:24.78,0:29:28.14,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}A short proof says why:\Neven a perfect generator,
Dialogue: 0,0:29:28.22,0:29:31.35,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}forced this way, cannot match the truth.
Dialogue: 0,0:29:31.73,0:29:34.15,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}So the budget goes on the crowd shelf.
Dialogue: 0,0:29:34.55,0:29:39.16,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}On the next test bench, a cruder\Nscore is fooled by such a spike.
Dialogue: 0,0:29:40.20,0:29:43.24,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Impose the budget on the\Ncrowd, during training.
Dialogue: 0,0:29:43.68,0:29:47.64,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Draw a batch of snapshots at\Nany {\c&H7AAF1B&}Gamma{\c&HF7F4F2&}, even without data.
Dialogue: 0,0:29:48.08,0:29:51.95,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Average their energy change\Nand square it: this penalty,
Dialogue: 0,0:29:52.03,0:29:56.17,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}the crowd loss, vanishes\Nonly when the crowd balances.
Dialogue: 0,0:29:56.91,0:29:58.11,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}But there is a trap.
Dialogue: 0,0:29:58.55,0:30:02.15,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Even for a balanced crowd,\Na batch's average wobbles;
Dialogue: 0,0:30:02.23,0:30:04.91,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}its square grows with the scatter.
Dialogue: 0,0:30:05.12,0:30:07.65,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}So training also squeezes the scatter,
Dialogue: 0,0:30:07.73,0:30:11.91,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}nudging every snapshot toward\Nbalance: toward the spike.
Dialogue: 0,0:30:12.53,0:30:15.00,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}It showed up while\Nbuilding this experiment.
Dialogue: 0,0:30:15.74,0:30:18.84,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}The fix: split the batch\Ninto two independent
Dialogue: 0,0:30:18.92,0:30:21.71,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}juries and multiply their averages.
Dialogue: 0,0:30:22.13,0:30:27.23,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}On average, the scatter drops out,\Nleaving only the crowd's imbalance.
Dialogue: 0,0:30:27.61,0:30:31.14,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Does it pay off? The next tests will tell.
Dialogue: 0,0:30:32.47,0:30:35.48,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}In three dimensions,\Nwhich budgets should generated
Dialogue: 0,0:30:35.56,0:30:39.28,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}fields meet on average,\Nand must their spread match too?
Dialogue: 0,0:30:44.54,0:30:48.50,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}New test bench: whole\Nfields, not three dials.
Dialogue: 0,0:30:48.95,0:30:53.17,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}A flat square of conducting fluid,\Nits edges wrapping around.
Dialogue: 0,0:30:53.52,0:30:56.52,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}In blue, the swirl of\Nthe {\c&HE58739&}flow{\c&HF7F4F2&}; in vermillion,
Dialogue: 0,0:30:56.60,0:30:59.54,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}the electric currents behind the {\c&H3468EB&}field{\c&HF7F4F2&}.
Dialogue: 0,0:31:00.28,0:31:04.03,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}One knob, {\c&H7AAF1B&}eta{\c&HF7F4F2&}: how fast the field smudges.
Dialogue: 0,0:31:04.24,0:31:07.22,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}But a flat world cannot sustain a dynamo.
Dialogue: 0,0:31:07.43,0:31:10.54,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Here a random forcing\Nkeeps the field alive:
Dialogue: 0,0:31:10.62,0:31:13.93,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}a training ground for laws, not a dynamo.
Dialogue: 0,0:31:14.97,0:31:19.33,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Every training field passes the\Nbox test: it is divergence-free.
Dialogue: 0,0:31:19.76,0:31:21.89,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Train a plain {\c&HF09CB4&}generator{\c&HF7F4F2&} on them.
Dialogue: 0,0:31:22.63,0:31:25.01,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Will its fields be divergence-free?
Dialogue: 0,0:31:28.75,0:31:29.67,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}No.
Dialogue: 0,0:31:29.75,0:31:32.83,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Now lines appear and\Nvanish inside the box.
Dialogue: 0,0:31:33.15,0:31:35.93,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Relative to the field's own variations,
Dialogue: 0,0:31:36.01,0:31:40.81,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}the divergence is zero point\Nfour one: a defect of order one.
Dialogue: 0,0:31:41.55,0:31:44.78,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}A soft penalty only lowers\Nit to zero point three
Dialogue: 0,0:31:44.86,0:31:48.71,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}four for the {\c&HE58739&}flow{\c&HF7F4F2&}, zero\Npoint one six for the field.
Dialogue: 0,0:31:48.97,0:31:53.06,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}And the magnetic energy comes out\Ntwo point seven times too high.
Dialogue: 0,0:31:53.46,0:31:56.80,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}This law belongs on the\Nshelf for every snapshot.
Dialogue: 0,0:31:57.84,0:32:00.72,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}First fix: draw a hill map, not arrows,
Dialogue: 0,0:32:00.80,0:32:04.56,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}and let the {\c&HE58739&}flow{\c&HF7F4F2&} run\Nalong its contour lines.
Dialogue: 0,0:32:05.02,0:32:07.58,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Where the lines crowd, it runs faster,
Dialogue: 0,0:32:07.66,0:32:11.68,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}so nothing piles up or leaks,\Nwhatever the landscape.
Dialogue: 0,0:32:12.26,0:32:16.20,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}The box agrees: lines in, lines out.
Dialogue: 0,0:32:16.94,0:32:21.01,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}So the {\c&HF09CB4&}machine{\c&HF7F4F2&} draws two hill maps,\Nfor the flow and the field:
Dialogue: 0,0:32:21.09,0:32:25.44,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}zero divergence by construction,\Nhowever badly it is trained.
Dialogue: 0,0:32:26.18,0:32:32.18,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Second fix, for any field: split it into\Nswirl plus slope, and delete the slope.
Dialogue: 0,0:32:33.24,0:32:38.65,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Wave by wave, remove the part of each\Narrow along the wave's own direction.
Dialogue: 0,0:32:39.39,0:32:44.26,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}The gauges fall to rounding error,\Ndown to ten to the minus seventeen.
Dialogue: 0,0:32:44.49,0:32:48.42,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Measured, though, with the very\Noperator used to enforce it.
Dialogue: 0,0:32:49.47,0:32:54.40,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Back to the plain {\c&HF09CB4&}generator{\c&HF7F4F2&}:\Nwhy does it miss, trained on clean data?
Dialogue: 0,0:32:55.14,0:32:57.75,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Follow the part of a\Nfield breaking the law.
Dialogue: 0,0:32:58.06,0:33:01.85,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}To bring it exactly to zero,\Nthe {\c&HF09CB4&}wind{\c&HF7F4F2&} must act as a spring
Dialogue: 0,0:33:01.93,0:33:06.27,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}that turns infinitely stiff as\Nthe transport clock runs out.
Dialogue: 0,0:33:07.01,0:33:12.39,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}A bounded network cannot, and a sampler\Nwith finite steps cannot follow either.
Dialogue: 0,0:33:12.82,0:33:17.77,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}A gap of order one remains:\Nalmost is not exactly.
Dialogue: 0,0:33:18.80,0:33:24.20,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Now the crowd law: on average,\Nenergy in equals energy out.
Dialogue: 0,0:33:24.58,0:33:28.77,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Three ways to impose it,\Nat settings of {\c&H7AAF1B&}eta{\c&HF7F4F2&} never seen.
Dialogue: 0,0:33:29.51,0:33:34.06,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}One: during training, the crowd\Nloss, with its two juries.
Dialogue: 0,0:33:34.33,0:33:36.54,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Against a control run of equal length,
Dialogue: 0,0:33:36.62,0:33:41.28,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}the {\c&H00A1ED&}distribution distance{\c&HF7F4F2&} drops from\Ntwo point five to zero point seven.
Dialogue: 0,0:33:41.53,0:33:43.89,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}The control itself gains nothing.
Dialogue: 0,0:33:44.63,0:33:48.87,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Two: after training,\Nreweight the generated crowd.
Dialogue: 0,0:33:49.28,0:33:52.40,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Where the model is worst,\Na poll of five hundred
Dialogue: 0,0:33:52.48,0:33:56.01,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}shrinks to one person,\Nand the crowd gets worse.
Dialogue: 0,0:33:56.75,0:34:00.70,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Three: rescale every\Nsnapshot onto the budget.
Dialogue: 0,0:34:01.14,0:34:04.28,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Each snapshot now dissipates\Nthe same: a spike,
Dialogue: 0,0:34:04.36,0:34:07.29,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}under a thousandth of the true spread.
Dialogue: 0,0:34:07.61,0:34:13.31,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Yet the same {\c&H00A1ED&}distribution distance{\c&HF7F4F2&},\Nbuilt on spectra, improves: it is fooled.
Dialogue: 0,0:34:13.68,0:34:18.78,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Only the spread gives the spike away.\NThe crowd loss shows no spike.
Dialogue: 0,0:34:19.52,0:34:22.55,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}One training seed each,\Ninterpolation only,
Dialogue: 0,0:34:22.63,0:34:25.70,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}every model far above the noise floor.
Dialogue: 0,0:34:26.74,0:34:29.15,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Back to the two shelves, now full.
Dialogue: 0,0:34:29.54,0:34:33.30,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}For every snapshot: zero\Ndivergence. Build it in,
Dialogue: 0,0:34:33.38,0:34:38.19,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}or project, and it holds exactly;\Na penalty only roughly.
Dialogue: 0,0:34:38.93,0:34:42.62,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}For the crowd: the energy budget.\NImpose it on the crowd
Dialogue: 0,0:34:42.70,0:34:47.33,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}during training. Reweighting afterwards\Nfails where the model is poor;
Dialogue: 0,0:34:47.41,0:34:50.70,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}forcing it onto each\Nsnapshot makes a spike.
Dialogue: 0,0:34:51.44,0:34:56.14,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}And the mirror? Each snapshot is\Nlopsided, so it is a crowd law,
Dialogue: 0,0:34:56.22,0:34:59.92,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}yet a network can carry\Nit exactly, built in.
Dialogue: 0,0:35:00.66,0:35:05.40,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}One rule sorts them all:\Nenforce each law where it is true.
Dialogue: 0,0:35:08.34,0:35:12.08,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Put the pieces together:\Nthe machine this study proposes.
Dialogue: 0,0:35:12.32,0:35:17.34,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}A {\c&HF09CB4&}wind{\c&HF7F4F2&} carries noise to a symmetric\Ncrowd of codes, mirror built in.
Dialogue: 0,0:35:17.70,0:35:23.33,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}A decoder turns each code into hill maps,\Nso every field is divergence-free.
Dialogue: 0,0:35:23.62,0:35:26.41,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}And the energy budget\Nis imposed on the crowd.
Dialogue: 0,0:35:27.15,0:35:31.25,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}It has not been built as a whole:\Nthe experiments tested pieces,
Dialogue: 0,0:35:31.33,0:35:36.76,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}on three dials and a flat sheet, with\Nsmall networks on an ordinary processor.
Dialogue: 0,0:35:37.50,0:35:43.03,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}One question remains: from snapshots,\Nhow well can we answer {\c&H00A1ED&}how often{\c&HF7F4F2&}?
Dialogue: 0,0:35:44.17,0:35:48.04,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}In the partners' solver snapshots,\Nwhich laws hold exactly,
Dialogue: 0,0:35:48.12,0:35:50.72,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}and under which discrete operator?
Dialogue: 0,0:35:54.77,0:35:58.20,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}An aside: the five statements\Nbehind the pictures.
Dialogue: 0,0:35:58.45,0:36:02.54,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Each is elementary; what\Nmatters is its exact scope.
Dialogue: 0,0:36:03.28,0:36:05.50,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}The first is almost too simple.
Dialogue: 0,0:36:05.85,0:36:11.78,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Suppose every field a {\c&HF09CB4&}decoder{\c&HF7F4F2&} can draw\Nobeys a linear law, like zero divergence.
Dialogue: 0,0:36:12.01,0:36:17.24,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Then every sample obeys it, whatever the\N{\c&HF09CB4&}crowd of codes{\c&HF7F4F2&}, however badly trained.
Dialogue: 0,0:36:17.98,0:36:19.85,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}The proof is one line.
Dialogue: 0,0:36:20.06,0:36:23.82,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}The work is in the decoder:\Nhill maps in two dimensions,
Dialogue: 0,0:36:23.90,0:36:27.65,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}a vector potential in three,\Nwith its gauge fixed.
Dialogue: 0,0:36:28.70,0:36:31.16,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Second: the spring, in full.
Dialogue: 0,0:36:31.76,0:36:36.78,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}If the data lie in a flat subspace,\Nthe ideal {\c&HF09CB4&}wind{\c&HF7F4F2&} splits in two.
Dialogue: 0,0:36:37.15,0:36:40.10,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Along it, a part a network can learn.
Dialogue: 0,0:36:40.38,0:36:46.38,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Across it, a pull back: the stray part,\Ndivided by the time left on the clock.
Dialogue: 0,0:36:47.29,0:36:51.81,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Followed exactly, that pull lands\Nevery sample in the subspace.
Dialogue: 0,0:36:52.45,0:36:55.09,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Trained perfectly, the plain generator,
Dialogue: 0,0:36:55.17,0:36:59.05,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}both projections and hill\Nmaps give the same crowd.
Dialogue: 0,0:36:59.79,0:37:03.58,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Cap the pull, and an\Norder-one stray part survives.
Dialogue: 0,0:37:04.09,0:37:09.92,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Now add a small, bounded error to the\Nexact pull. Does a stray part survive?
Dialogue: 0,0:37:13.16,0:37:15.57,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}No. The pull erases it.
Dialogue: 0,0:37:15.94,0:37:18.97,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}So the plain generator's\Nzero point four one
Dialogue: 0,0:37:19.05,0:37:22.41,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}reads as a missing pull,\Nnot a small error.
Dialogue: 0,0:37:23.15,0:37:26.96,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}And the projected flow's tiny\Nresidue? Single precision.
Dialogue: 0,0:37:28.00,0:37:33.89,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Third: penalties. A common one tests the\N{\c&HF09CB4&}model's guess{\c&HF7F4F2&} of where its path will end.
Dialogue: 0,0:37:34.32,0:37:38.86,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}With the ideal wind, that guess\Naverages the data that could lie ahead.
Dialogue: 0,0:37:39.60,0:37:43.30,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}For a linear law, an average\Nof lawful fields is lawful:
Dialogue: 0,0:37:43.38,0:37:47.42,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}the penalty vanishes at the\Noptimum, and moves nothing.
Dialogue: 0,0:37:47.89,0:37:52.72,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Off the optimum, in a real network,\Nit holds the law only roughly.
Dialogue: 0,0:37:53.46,0:37:59.43,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}For a curved law, that fails: two points\Non a circle average to a point inside.
Dialogue: 0,0:37:59.75,0:38:04.77,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}This is the Jensen gap: now the\Npenalty biases the optimum itself.
Dialogue: 0,0:38:05.80,0:38:08.81,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Fourth: the mirror, as a theorem.
Dialogue: 0,0:38:09.30,0:38:14.72,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Make the {\c&HF09CB4&}wind{\c&HF7F4F2&} equivariant: mirror\Na point, and its arrow mirrors too.
Dialogue: 0,0:38:15.10,0:38:18.14,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Start from noise that looks\Nthe same in the mirror.
Dialogue: 0,0:38:18.88,0:38:21.29,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Then every path has a mirror twin.
Dialogue: 0,0:38:21.37,0:38:24.62,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}So the generated crowd is\Nexactly symmetric at every
Dialogue: 0,0:38:24.70,0:38:28.16,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}{\c&H7AAF1B&}knob setting{\c&HF7F4F2&}, even with\Nlopsided or missing data.
Dialogue: 0,0:38:28.90,0:38:32.11,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}If every true crowd is\Nsymmetric, averaging a wind
Dialogue: 0,0:38:32.19,0:38:35.61,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}with its mirror never\Nraises the expected loss.
Dialogue: 0,0:38:35.91,0:38:39.39,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}The cost: two network\Npasses instead of one.
Dialogue: 0,0:38:40.13,0:38:45.65,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}In the VKS cylinder, a half-turn swapping\Nthe two impellers is a second symmetry.
Dialogue: 0,0:38:46.11,0:38:49.00,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}If their speeds differ,\Nit also flips the sign
Dialogue: 0,0:38:49.08,0:38:52.12,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}of that difference, the {\c&H7AAF1B&}forcing asymmetry{\c&HF7F4F2&}.
Dialogue: 0,0:38:52.54,0:38:55.95,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}The same proof covers it, untested so far.
Dialogue: 0,0:38:57.00,0:38:59.83,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Last, crowd laws. The equations give any
Dialogue: 0,0:38:59.91,0:39:03.36,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}quantity a rate of\Nchange at each snapshot.
Dialogue: 0,0:39:03.75,0:39:07.55,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}If the crowd is stationary,\Nthat rate {\c&H00A1ED&}averages to zero{\c&HF7F4F2&},
Dialogue: 0,0:39:07.63,0:39:11.49,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}for every quantity;\Nthe energy budget is one case.
Dialogue: 0,0:39:12.23,0:39:16.01,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}The proof: let every\Nsnapshot run for a moment.
Dialogue: 0,0:39:16.65,0:39:22.17,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}The crowd looks the same, so no\Naverage moves: the mean rate is zero.
Dialogue: 0,0:39:22.91,0:39:26.42,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Necessary, not sufficient:\Na crowd parked on an
Dialogue: 0,0:39:26.50,0:39:30.19,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}unstable fixed point\Npasses every such test.
Dialogue: 0,0:39:30.30,0:39:32.10,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}So does an unstable loop.
Dialogue: 0,0:39:32.61,0:39:35.87,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}The fit to data must\Ntherefore stay in the loss.
Dialogue: 0,0:39:37.00,0:39:40.03,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Can we simulate only one\Nsign of forcing asymmetry,
Dialogue: 0,0:39:40.11,0:39:43.06,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}and let the impeller\Nswap supply the other?
Dialogue: 0,0:39:45.90,0:39:49.50,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Earlier, a {\c&H00A1ED&}distribution distance{\c&HF7F4F2&}\Nmeant moving sand.
Dialogue: 0,0:39:49.88,0:39:53.61,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}The experiments mainly use\Na cousin, built from pairs.
Dialogue: 0,0:39:53.86,0:39:56.07,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Each dot is a snapshot.
Dialogue: 0,0:39:56.81,0:40:00.66,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Average every distance across\Nthe two crowds, and double it.
Dialogue: 0,0:40:01.05,0:40:04.12,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Subtract the average\Ndistance within each crowd.
Dialogue: 0,0:40:04.41,0:40:06.67,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Zero means the same law.
Dialogue: 0,0:40:08.90,0:40:10.85,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Reweighting, up close.
Dialogue: 0,0:40:11.24,0:40:14.10,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}The {\c&HF09CB4&}model's{\c&HF7F4F2&} crowd misses\Nits target average.
Dialogue: 0,0:40:14.48,0:40:19.51,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Leave every snapshot in place;\Nchange only how much each counts.
Dialogue: 0,0:40:20.25,0:40:22.09,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Many weightings hit the target.
Dialogue: 0,0:40:22.44,0:40:27.50,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}The least distorting one is an exponential\Ntilt, known as the I-projection.
Dialogue: 0,0:40:28.24,0:40:33.48,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Picture crowds as points: those with\Nthe right average lie on a line.
Dialogue: 0,0:40:33.74,0:40:36.82,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}The tilted crowd is the\Nfoot of the perpendicular.
Dialogue: 0,0:40:37.25,0:40:42.63,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Any other fix pays the tilt's cost\Nplus an extra, as in Pythagoras.
Dialogue: 0,0:40:42.94,0:40:48.65,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}And nothing moves: the tilted crowd\Nlives only where the model's crowd was.
Dialogue: 0,0:40:49.70,0:40:52.33,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}How much is left of a reweighted crowd?
Dialogue: 0,0:40:52.45,0:40:54.61,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Its {\c&H00A1ED&}effective sample size{\c&HF7F4F2&} says.
Dialogue: 0,0:40:54.97,0:40:57.38,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Equal weights: the whole crowd.
Dialogue: 0,0:40:57.70,0:41:00.55,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}One giant weight: one person.
Dialogue: 0,0:41:01.29,0:41:06.69,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}On the flat sheet, at the smallest test\N{\c&H7AAF1B&}eta{\c&HF7F4F2&}, the poll keeps ninety-nine percent.
Dialogue: 0,0:41:07.10,0:41:12.35,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}At the three largest, it bottoms out:\None snapshot in five hundred.
Dialogue: 0,0:41:13.09,0:41:16.40,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Rank the values by how far\Nthe {\c&HF09CB4&}model's{\c&HF7F4F2&} budget was off:
Dialogue: 0,0:41:16.48,0:41:19.45,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}the poll shrinks in nearly the same order.
Dialogue: 0,0:41:19.99,0:41:22.99,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Where the poll survives,\Nlittle needed fixing.
Dialogue: 0,0:41:23.35,0:41:26.31,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Where much needed fixing, it collapses.
Dialogue: 0,0:41:27.05,0:41:30.79,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}On the three dials, too,\Nreweighting improved no crowd:
Dialogue: 0,0:41:30.87,0:41:34.81,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}one snapshot weighed\Nthirty-three times the average.
Dialogue: 0,0:41:35.87,0:41:38.81,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Picture a {\c&HF09CB4&}model{\c&HF7F4F2&} that never\Nvisits the bridge between
Dialogue: 0,0:41:38.89,0:41:42.06,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}the two polarities,\Nwhere every crossing happens.
Dialogue: 0,0:41:42.80,0:41:45.37,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Can reweighting bring\Nback the {\c&H00A1ED&}crossing rate{\c&HF7F4F2&}?
Dialogue: 0,0:41:48.61,0:41:52.03,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}No. Rice reads the rate inside the strip,
Dialogue: 0,0:41:52.11,0:41:57.23,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}and the strip is empty. Zero,\Ntimes any weight, is zero.
Dialogue: 0,0:41:57.69,0:42:02.13,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Push harder, and the weight piles\Nonto a few snapshots at the edge.
Dialogue: 0,0:42:02.87,0:42:06.15,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Reweighting cannot visit\Nplaces the model never went.
Dialogue: 0,0:42:06.54,0:42:11.99,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}The PhD plan's risk table agrees:\Nreweighting alone is no fallback.
Dialogue: 0,0:42:13.04,0:42:15.03,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}One test {\c&H7AAF1B&}eta{\c&HF7F4F2&} at a time.
Dialogue: 0,0:42:15.49,0:42:18.94,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}At the largest, the base\N{\c&HF09CB4&}model's{\c&HF7F4F2&} snapshots dissipate,
Dialogue: 0,0:42:19.02,0:42:22.08,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}on average, over twice the energy fed in.
Dialogue: 0,0:42:22.57,0:42:25.30,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}The crowd loss cuts the\N{\c&H00A1ED&}distribution distance{\c&HF7F4F2&}
Dialogue: 0,0:42:25.38,0:42:28.22,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}from seven point seven to two point two.
Dialogue: 0,0:42:28.96,0:42:32.56,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}At the smallest, already good: no change.
Dialogue: 0,0:42:33.01,0:42:34.94,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}The control run stays put.
Dialogue: 0,0:42:35.68,0:42:38.00,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Same values where the poll collapsed:
Dialogue: 0,0:42:38.08,0:42:41.26,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}training moved the crowd;\Nweights could not.
Dialogue: 0,0:42:41.58,0:42:43.01,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}One seed, though.
Dialogue: 0,0:42:44.14,0:42:46.87,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}If the crowd loss fails,\Nwhat is the fallback,
Dialogue: 0,0:42:46.95,0:42:50.22,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}since reweighting fails\Nwhere the model is poor?
Dialogue: 0,0:42:55.27,0:42:59.40,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}The toll counter is back:\Nevery car is now {\c&HF09CB4&}generated{\c&HF7F4F2&}.
Dialogue: 0,0:42:59.78,0:43:02.84,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}They come from the machine\Nwith the built-in mirror:
Dialogue: 0,0:43:02.92,0:43:05.01,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}twenty thousand at each of forty-one
Dialogue: 0,0:43:05.09,0:43:08.01,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}settings of {\c&H7AAF1B&}Gamma{\c&HF7F4F2&},\Nmost unseen in training.
Dialogue: 0,0:43:08.49,0:43:12.58,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Rice, with the equations,\Nturns them into a {\c&H00A1ED&}crossing rate{\c&HF7F4F2&}.
Dialogue: 0,0:43:13.32,0:43:16.44,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Against the counted truth,\Nfar from the threshold,
Dialogue: 0,0:43:16.52,0:43:19.47,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}the {\c&H00A1ED&}cumulative error{\c&HF7F4F2&} is fourteen percent.
Dialogue: 0,0:43:19.91,0:43:23.45,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Setting by setting, the miss\Naverages seventeen percent,
Dialogue: 0,0:43:23.53,0:43:26.90,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}and reaches sixty-five\Npercent at two settings.
Dialogue: 0,0:43:27.47,0:43:31.45,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Near the threshold, the error\Nclimbs to forty-two percent.
Dialogue: 0,0:43:32.19,0:43:34.64,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}One model, one training seed.
Dialogue: 0,0:43:34.90,0:43:38.96,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Rice needs the equations,\Nand gives a rate, not waiting times.
Dialogue: 0,0:43:39.55,0:43:44.08,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}So, the study's third question:\Ncan a machine tell us {\c&H00A1ED&}how often{\c&HF7F4F2&}?
Dialogue: 0,0:43:44.43,0:43:50.43,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}For crossings on three dials: far from the\Nthreshold, roughly yes; near it, not yet.
Dialogue: 0,0:43:52.57,0:43:57.48,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Zoom in. The truth is silent\Nup to zero point eight six.
Dialogue: 0,0:43:57.84,0:44:00.60,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}But the machine changes\Nsmoothly with {\c&H7AAF1B&}Gamma{\c&HF7F4F2&}.
Dialogue: 0,0:44:01.34,0:44:05.41,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Will its crossing rate switch\Non early, late, or on time?
Dialogue: 0,0:44:09.15,0:44:12.07,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Early and late, but never on time.
Dialogue: 0,0:44:12.56,0:44:15.48,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Below the threshold,\Nit leaks false crossings:
Dialogue: 0,0:44:15.56,0:44:18.30,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}up to a tenth of the rate just above.
Dialogue: 0,0:44:18.74,0:44:24.48,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}At zero point eight six five, it is late:\Nless than a sixth of the counted rate.
Dialogue: 0,0:44:25.00,0:44:28.21,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Just above, it overshoots:\Nfive crossings per
Dialogue: 0,0:44:28.29,0:44:31.99,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}thousand time units,\Nagainst three point four.
Dialogue: 0,0:44:33.04,0:44:36.46,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Why? Picture the machine as a stiff sheet:
Dialogue: 0,0:44:36.54,0:44:40.80,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}as {\c&H7AAF1B&}Gamma{\c&HF7F4F2&} turns, its crowd\Nchanges only so fast.
Dialogue: 0,0:44:41.12,0:44:47.12,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}The truth climbs a steep ramp: just above\Nzero point eight six, the corridor opens.
Dialogue: 0,0:44:48.59,0:44:52.59,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}The sheet rounds the corner:\Ntoo early, then too low.
Dialogue: 0,0:44:52.96,0:44:58.74,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}The data show both: early strays in the\Ncorridor, then too few at the threshold.
Dialogue: 0,0:44:59.48,0:45:05.05,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}A theorem: at a true jump, a smooth\Nmachine misses by at least half of it.
Dialogue: 0,0:45:05.46,0:45:09.86,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Unless it is very supple,\Nor told which state the system is in.
Dialogue: 0,0:45:10.60,0:45:16.60,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Here, a ramp, not a jump, and one seed:\Nconsistent with the theorem, not proof.
Dialogue: 0,0:45:17.90,0:45:20.52,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Nor does the theorem\Nexplain the overshoot.
Dialogue: 0,0:45:21.97,0:45:25.84,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}The crowd loss, on three\Ndials, at unseen settings.
Dialogue: 0,0:45:26.31,0:45:29.77,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}On the good machine,\Nit halves the crowd-law residuals,
Dialogue: 0,0:45:29.85,0:45:32.24,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}even for laws it never enforced.
Dialogue: 0,0:45:32.73,0:45:38.54,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}But the spread of energy changes shrinks\Nby a quarter: a faint echo of the spike.
Dialogue: 0,0:45:39.28,0:45:42.61,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}On a deliberately biased\Nmachine, it fails:
Dialogue: 0,0:45:42.69,0:45:46.03,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}one run diverged, the other got worse.
Dialogue: 0,0:45:46.45,0:45:48.86,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}On the crossing rate, with one seed:
Dialogue: 0,0:45:48.94,0:45:53.01,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}slightly better near the\Nthreshold, worse far from it.
Dialogue: 0,0:45:54.44,0:45:57.44,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Now the strictest judge: the {\c&H00A1ED&}noise floor{\c&HF7F4F2&}.
Dialogue: 0,0:45:57.66,0:46:00.64,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}On three dials, the best\Nmachine sits at about
Dialogue: 0,0:46:00.72,0:46:03.99,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}twice its floor;\Nbut three numbers are easy.
Dialogue: 0,0:46:04.28,0:46:08.29,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}On the flat sheet of plasma,\Nevery model's spectra miss
Dialogue: 0,0:46:08.37,0:46:12.87,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}far more than two data samples\Ndiffer: at best, sixfold.
Dialogue: 0,0:46:13.61,0:46:19.61,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Two seeds on the dials, one on the sheet;\Nthe PhD plan asks for at least three.
Dialogue: 0,0:46:20.17,0:46:23.01,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Every test sits between trained settings.
Dialogue: 0,0:46:24.44,0:46:28.37,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}The scoreboard: eight mechanisms,\Neach with a verdict.
Dialogue: 0,0:46:28.80,0:46:32.40,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}For every snapshot:\Nhill maps and projection,
Dialogue: 0,0:46:32.48,0:46:35.43,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}exact; the penalty, approximate.
Dialogue: 0,0:46:35.97,0:46:40.59,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}For the crowd: the mirror,\Nexact; the crowd loss,
Dialogue: 0,0:46:40.67,0:46:44.94,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}useful but not clean; the rest, failures.
Dialogue: 0,0:46:45.68,0:46:47.84,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Back to the study's three questions.
Dialogue: 0,0:46:48.17,0:46:51.59,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}One: between the dots,\Nthe shapes come out right,
Dialogue: 0,0:46:51.67,0:46:54.65,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}but no model reaches its {\c&H00A1ED&}noise floor{\c&HF7F4F2&}.
Dialogue: 0,0:46:55.16,0:47:00.97,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Two: each law where it holds,\Nexactly yes, {\c&H00A1ED&}on average{\c&HF7F4F2&} partly.
Dialogue: 0,0:47:01.52,0:47:07.48,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Three, {\c&H00A1ED&}how often{\c&HF7F4F2&}: roughly right far\Nfrom the threshold, poor near it.
Dialogue: 0,0:47:08.22,0:47:09.42,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Three lessons.
Dialogue: 0,0:47:09.84,0:47:12.43,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Exact laws: build them in.
Dialogue: 0,0:47:12.84,0:47:17.74,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Crowd laws: impose them on the\Ncrowd, never on each snapshot.
Dialogue: 0,0:47:18.17,0:47:22.70,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}And judge every result against the\Nnoise floor, with enough seeds.
Dialogue: 0,0:47:24.54,0:47:27.76,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Which settings should get runs\Nlong enough to count reversals,
Dialogue: 0,0:47:27.84,0:47:30.04,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}so a predicted rate can be checked?
Dialogue: 0,0:47:32.47,0:47:34.74,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}How wide should Rice's strip be?
Dialogue: 0,0:47:35.19,0:47:40.13,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Widen it: the estimate blurs,\Nits bias growing like the width squared.
Dialogue: 0,0:47:40.46,0:47:45.21,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Narrow it: the estimate jitters,\Nas fewer snapshots land inside.
Dialogue: 0,0:47:45.64,0:47:50.59,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}The three-dial experiment used\Nsnapshots within zero point two of zero.
Dialogue: 0,0:47:51.33,0:47:55.09,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}With endless snapshots,\Nwhy not shrink it to nothing?
Dialogue: 0,0:47:55.50,0:48:00.55,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}The exact formula assumes a smooth\Ncloud. A cosine has only a thin ring,
Dialogue: 0,0:48:00.63,0:48:04.73,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}yet the formula still finds\Ntwo crossings per period.
Dialogue: 0,0:48:05.47,0:48:09.99,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Chaos may lack a smooth cloud too,\Nso nothing guarantees the limit:
Dialogue: 0,0:48:10.07,0:48:15.48,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}on the three dials, the only test so\Nfar, the answer depends on the strip.
Dialogue: 0,0:48:16.74,0:48:20.29,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}To count reversals,\Nnot crossings, raise the bar:
Dialogue: 0,0:48:20.37,0:48:25.75,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}a reversal must run from above some\Nlevel to below its mirror image.
Dialogue: 0,0:48:25.97,0:48:27.72,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}So the level's {\c&H00A1ED&}crossing rate{\c&HF7F4F2&},
Dialogue: 0,0:48:27.80,0:48:31.68,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}which Rice reads from snapshots,\Ncaps the reversal rate.
Dialogue: 0,0:48:32.42,0:48:34.95,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Wobbles across the level loosen the cap.
Dialogue: 0,0:48:35.49,0:48:39.35,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}The conjecture: if a flip\Nlasts far longer than a wobble,
Dialogue: 0,0:48:39.43,0:48:43.01,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}the cap is nearly exact\Non a plateau of levels.
Dialogue: 0,0:48:43.75,0:48:49.38,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}The bet, written before the data, from the\NPhD plan: on the staircase's first step,
Dialogue: 0,0:48:49.46,0:48:54.74,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}the cap comes within thirty percent\Nof the counted rate on that plateau.
Dialogue: 0,0:48:56.28,0:49:01.51,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Confidence: medium; the cap\Nis proved, the plateau is not.
Dialogue: 0,0:49:01.99,0:49:05.60,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Ask first: how wide must that plateau be?
Dialogue: 0,0:49:06.84,0:49:09.22,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}One more limit, a proved one.
Dialogue: 0,0:49:09.50,0:49:13.92,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}These two smooth random\Nsignals share one snapshot law,
Dialogue: 0,0:49:14.00,0:49:17.44,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}value and speed alike: same Rice rate.
Dialogue: 0,0:49:17.82,0:49:22.84,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Yet one crosses zero like a metronome,\Nthe other at uneven gaps.
Dialogue: 0,0:49:23.58,0:49:28.31,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}On three dials, Gissinger found a\Nshape in the waits between flips:
Dialogue: 0,0:49:28.39,0:49:33.67,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}a comb of short, quantized waits,\Nthen a memoryless exponential tail.
Dialogue: 0,0:49:34.41,0:49:38.04,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}No shoebox holds that shape;\Nit takes the movie.
Dialogue: 0,0:49:38.50,0:49:40.65,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}The open question: can short runs,
Dialogue: 0,0:49:40.73,0:49:45.33,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}launched from generated states in\Nthe corridor, supply the waits?
Dialogue: 0,0:49:46.37,0:49:49.26,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}The staircase's first\Nstep gets a second round.
Dialogue: 0,0:49:49.49,0:49:51.52,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Three seeds, not two.
Dialogue: 0,0:49:51.74,0:49:55.72,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}A second model, distilled\Nfrom VKS simulations.
Dialogue: 0,0:49:56.03,0:49:59.69,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}And the impeller-swap mirror,\Nwhich also flips {\c&H7AAF1B&}Gamma{\c&HF7F4F2&}.
Dialogue: 0,0:50:00.43,0:50:06.43,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Four lamps must light: lopsidedness,\Nunenforced laws, Rice, the level bound.
Dialogue: 0,0:50:09.02,0:50:13.70,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}The bet: unenforced residuals\Nhalve again, roughly. Last time,
Dialogue: 0,0:50:13.78,0:50:19.34,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}halving meant one point nine eight,\Nagainst a bar of two: a toss-up.
Dialogue: 0,0:50:19.77,0:50:21.63,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Confidence: medium.
Dialogue: 0,0:50:22.08,0:50:25.90,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}One catch: a perfectly\Nsymmetric model lands above the
Dialogue: 0,0:50:25.98,0:50:30.44,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}{\c&H00A1ED&}noise floor{\c&HF7F4F2&} nearly half the time;\Nthe floor is its average.
Dialogue: 0,0:50:30.52,0:50:34.81,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Ask first: should the first\Nlamp use a statistical test?
Dialogue: 0,0:50:36.14,0:50:40.69,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Beyond how often: for VKS,\Nshould we also target waiting times,
Dialogue: 0,0:50:40.77,0:50:44.42,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}with short solver runs\Nfrom generated states?
Dialogue: 0,0:50:49.47,0:50:52.78,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}So far, every test ran\Non toy models. Before
Dialogue: 0,0:50:52.86,0:50:56.51,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}real dynamos, back to\Nthe map of the field.
Dialogue: 0,0:50:56.71,0:51:01.76,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Zoom into its thin data-driven\Nlane: it opens into four tracks.
Dialogue: 0,0:51:02.50,0:51:07.65,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}A laboratory dynamo sets every\Ntrack four tests: three dimensions,
Dialogue: 0,0:51:07.73,0:51:13.73,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}walls with iron and a vacuum, a tiny\N{\c&H7AAF1B&}magnetic Prandtl number{\c&HF7F4F2&}, and rare flips.
Dialogue: 0,0:51:15.60,0:51:21.03,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}First track: fast surrogates,\Nnetworks trained to imitate a solver.
Dialogue: 0,0:51:21.35,0:51:26.70,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Some carry the equations in their loss;\Nothers learn maps between whole fields.
Dialogue: 0,0:51:27.12,0:51:32.15,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}For conducting fluids, they now reach\Nflat, two-dimensional turbulence.
Dialogue: 0,0:51:32.27,0:51:35.49,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}For the Earth, recall the\Ntwenty twenty-two study:
Dialogue: 0,0:51:35.57,0:51:39.52,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}the methods it tried could\Nnot reliably foresee a flip.
Dialogue: 0,0:51:40.26,0:51:44.04,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Their weak spot: trained to\Nminimize a mean-square error,
Dialogue: 0,0:51:44.12,0:51:47.35,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}ensembles of them\Nregress to the mean flow.
Dialogue: 0,0:51:47.59,0:51:51.21,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}A cloud with two polarities\Ncollapses to its middle.
Dialogue: 0,0:51:52.27,0:51:54.35,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Second track: {\c&HF09CB4&}generative models{\c&HF7F4F2&},
Dialogue: 0,0:51:54.43,0:51:58.06,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}which learn the whole cloud,\Nnot just its middle.
Dialogue: 0,0:51:58.53,0:52:01.50,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Adversarial networks\Nfirst, then diffusion,
Dialogue: 0,0:52:01.58,0:52:05.18,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}then flow matching,\Nthe family the study used.
Dialogue: 0,0:52:05.63,0:52:09.89,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}They now dream three-dimensional\Nturbulence, global weather ensembles,
Dialogue: 0,0:52:09.97,0:52:13.28,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}and steady turbulent\Nstates for chosen {\c&H7AAF1B&}settings{\c&HF7F4F2&}.
Dialogue: 0,0:52:14.02,0:52:17.58,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Trained on dynamo\Nsimulations? We found none.
Dialogue: 0,0:52:19.44,0:52:24.54,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Third track: making samples\Nobey the laws. Four families.
Dialogue: 0,0:52:24.93,0:52:29.19,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Hard: build the law into the network,\Nsay a potential whose curl
Dialogue: 0,0:52:29.27,0:52:33.61,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}has no divergence, or project\Neach sample while drawing it.
Dialogue: 0,0:52:33.99,0:52:38.91,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Soft: a penalty in the loss,\Nobeyed only approximately.
Dialogue: 0,0:52:39.14,0:52:42.11,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}On the {\c&H00A1ED&}distribution{\c&HF7F4F2&}: constrain the crowd,
Dialogue: 0,0:52:42.19,0:52:46.92,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}by its covariance, or by the\Nequation a steady cloud obeys.
Dialogue: 0,0:52:47.36,0:52:52.20,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}And symmetry: an equivariant\Nflow gives a symmetric cloud.
Dialogue: 0,0:52:53.34,0:52:57.22,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}A twenty twenty-six preprint\Ncompared ways to impose zero
Dialogue: 0,0:52:57.30,0:53:01.46,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}divergence on flat flows:\Nno single strategy dominates.
Dialogue: 0,0:53:01.90,0:53:06.95,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}In three dimensions, we found two\Nexactly divergence-free generators,
Dialogue: 0,0:53:07.03,0:53:09.93,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}both preprints: periodic-box turbulence,
Dialogue: 0,0:53:10.01,0:53:13.69,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}and a current-free field\Nin the Sun's corona.
Dialogue: 0,0:53:13.78,0:53:18.50,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}None handles walls, a vacuum\Noutside and iron inside.
Dialogue: 0,0:53:19.54,0:53:24.78,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Fourth track: data assimilation,\Nmerging a model with a few measurements.
Dialogue: 0,0:53:25.12,0:53:30.49,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Learned priors came in through compressed\Nlatent spaces, then diffusion models.
Dialogue: 0,0:53:30.66,0:53:36.05,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}And a library now plugs deep networks into\NKalman filters and variational methods.
Dialogue: 0,0:53:36.79,0:53:40.74,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}We found no diffusion prior used\Nthis way for conducting fluids.
Dialogue: 0,0:53:41.12,0:53:43.71,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Near a flip, the answer\Nhas two polarities,
Dialogue: 0,0:53:43.79,0:53:47.92,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}and a prior that ignores the\Nmirror could bias it toward one.
Dialogue: 0,0:53:48.97,0:53:52.99,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Now the rings: papers\Nco-signed by the supervisors.
Dialogue: 0,0:53:53.71,0:53:59.15,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Dynamo side: Taylor–Green dynamos pushed\Nto small {\c&H7AAF1B&}magnetic Prandtl numbers{\c&HF7F4F2&},
Dialogue: 0,0:53:59.23,0:54:04.34,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}and the VKS tank with its iron\Nimpellers, simulated in two codes.
Dialogue: 0,0:54:04.45,0:54:08.54,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}In twenty twenty-six, both\Ncodes produced VKS reversals,
Dialogue: 0,0:54:08.62,0:54:12.89,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}and a four-dial model\Nreproduces their main features.
Dialogue: 0,0:54:13.66,0:54:17.49,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Learning side: autoencoder\Nlatent spaces read through
Dialogue: 0,0:54:17.57,0:54:21.36,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}classical flow modes,\Nvon Kármán flow included.
Dialogue: 0,0:54:21.65,0:54:24.44,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}And assimilation and\Ndiffusion models built
Dialogue: 0,0:54:24.52,0:54:27.78,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}on deep networks,\Noutside conducting fluids.
Dialogue: 0,0:54:28.92,0:54:31.96,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Three ingredients are in hand.\NBut in what we found,
Dialogue: 0,0:54:32.04,0:54:36.76,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}none of the four has published a\N{\c&HF09CB4&}generative model{\c&HF7F4F2&} trained on dynamo data.
Dialogue: 0,0:54:37.80,0:54:40.49,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Where the {\c&HF09CB4&}generative{\c&HF7F4F2&} and\Nconstraint tracks cross
Dialogue: 0,0:54:40.57,0:54:43.32,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}the dynamo tracks, we found the map empty.
Dialogue: 0,0:54:43.80,0:54:47.18,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}An independent thesis\Nproposal aims at that spot:
Dialogue: 0,0:54:47.26,0:54:50.76,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}five gaps, each beside\Nits nearest neighbour.
Dialogue: 0,0:54:51.50,0:54:54.59,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}One: a generator of dynamo fields.
Dialogue: 0,0:54:55.18,0:54:58.39,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Two: {\c&HE58739&}velocity{\c&HF7F4F2&} and\Nmagnetic field with exactly
Dialogue: 0,0:54:58.47,0:55:03.10,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}zero divergence, in a walled\Ncylinder with iron and vacuum.
Dialogue: 0,0:55:03.93,0:55:07.39,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Three: energy budgets\Nimposed on the {\c&H00A1ED&}distribution{\c&HF7F4F2&},
Dialogue: 0,0:55:07.47,0:55:11.40,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}with no target value,\Neven where there are no data.
Dialogue: 0,0:55:11.52,0:55:16.11,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Four: the flip rate read from\Nsnapshots with Rice's formula.
Dialogue: 0,0:55:16.58,0:55:20.95,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Five: one generator that\Ncrosses the dynamo threshold.
Dialogue: 0,0:55:22.09,0:55:27.38,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}The study tested pieces on toy cases:\Nexact potentials on a flat sheet,
Dialogue: 0,0:55:27.46,0:55:32.40,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}a budget imposed without data,\NRice's formula on three dials.
Dialogue: 0,0:55:33.44,0:55:37.72,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}The field moves fast: a twenty twenty-six\Nconference paper claims to be the
Dialogue: 0,0:55:37.80,0:55:42.56,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}first to recognize a trade-off between\Nphysical and {\c&H00A1ED&}distributional{\c&HF7F4F2&} accuracy.
Dialogue: 0,0:55:42.98,0:55:46.04,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}The study's lesson,\Nnever force a crowd law
Dialogue: 0,0:55:46.12,0:55:49.67,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}on each snapshot,\Nmust be placed against it.
Dialogue: 0,0:55:50.41,0:55:53.55,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Not claimed: waiting times between flips,
Dialogue: 0,0:55:53.63,0:55:59.63,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}sodium's tiny {\c&H7AAF1B&}magnetic Prandtl number{\c&HF7F4F2&},\Nthe Earth's core, real laboratory data.
Dialogue: 0,0:56:00.56,0:56:03.75,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Each empty spot is a\Nsearch result, not a proof,
Dialogue: 0,0:56:03.83,0:56:06.83,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}to be checked again before every paper.
Dialogue: 0,0:56:08.37,0:56:11.87,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}First VKS data: should\Nthe generator train on the
Dialogue: 0,0:56:11.95,0:56:15.85,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}LATU periodic grid,\Nor the SFEMaNS cylinder mesh?
Dialogue: 0,0:56:20.90,0:56:22.38,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Back to the staircase.
Dialogue: 0,0:56:22.75,0:56:26.10,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}The first two steps are\Nlit by small experiments:
Dialogue: 0,0:56:26.18,0:56:28.81,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}three numbers, then a flat sheet.
Dialogue: 0,0:56:29.28,0:56:33.35,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}The third, the twisting\Nbox, has only a preview.
Dialogue: 0,0:56:34.09,0:56:36.71,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}From there up, every step is a bet,
Dialogue: 0,0:56:36.79,0:56:42.28,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}written down before the data exist,\Nin an independent PhD proposal.
Dialogue: 0,0:56:43.34,0:56:46.81,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}To climb, the study first\Nbuilt a factory for the data.
Dialogue: 0,0:56:47.09,0:56:49.35,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}A recipe card sets the {\c&H7AAF1B&}knobs{\c&HF7F4F2&}.
Dialogue: 0,0:56:49.56,0:56:54.24,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Ovens run one simulation per setting,\Non a laptop or a cluster.
Dialogue: 0,0:56:54.75,0:56:57.59,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Each result is sealed in a labelled jar:
Dialogue: 0,0:56:57.67,0:57:01.37,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}a unique number,\Nthe code version, a checksum.
Dialogue: 0,0:57:02.11,0:57:06.88,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}A ledger records every jar, a menu\Nsplits them for training and testing,
Dialogue: 0,0:57:06.96,0:57:10.52,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}the {\c&HF09CB4&}chef{\c&HF7F4F2&} learns, and a\Ncritic scores the result.
Dialogue: 0,0:57:11.26,0:57:14.53,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}It all runs end to end\Non an ordinary processor,
Dialogue: 0,0:57:14.61,0:57:18.23,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}never yet on a graphics\Ncard or a real cluster.
Dialogue: 0,0:57:18.81,0:57:23.42,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}A grey tag names the file\Neach empty frame awaits.
Dialogue: 0,0:57:24.47,0:57:27.66,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}First product off the\Nline: the flat sheet,
Dialogue: 0,0:57:27.74,0:57:31.11,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}eleven {\c&H7AAF1B&}diffusivities{\c&HF7F4F2&}, sixteen runs each.
Dialogue: 0,0:57:31.38,0:57:34.57,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}The bet, a check on the\Ndata: at every setting,
Dialogue: 0,0:57:34.65,0:57:39.99,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}the {\c&H00A1ED&}average dissipation{\c&HF7F4F2&} matches the\Ninjected power, within five percent.
Dialogue: 0,0:57:42.44,0:57:46.27,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Open one oven: the solver\Nkeeps {\c&HE58739&}flow{\c&HF7F4F2&} and field as waves,
Dialogue: 0,0:57:46.35,0:57:48.99,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}where a curl is a multiplication.
Dialogue: 0,0:57:49.37,0:57:53.57,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Products are easier on the grid:\Neach step hops over and back.
Dialogue: 0,0:57:53.92,0:57:59.10,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}It trims the outer third of the waves,\Nso products cannot fold back as fakes.
Dialogue: 0,0:57:59.32,0:58:02.67,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}A projection keeps both\Nfields divergence-free;
Dialogue: 0,0:58:02.75,0:58:05.34,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}the smudging is solved exactly.
Dialogue: 0,0:58:06.08,0:58:08.02,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}One step at sixty-four cubed:
Dialogue: 0,0:58:08.10,0:58:12.28,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}eight hundredths of a second,\Non an ordinary processor.
Dialogue: 0,0:58:12.62,0:58:15.71,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}The memory peak, about\Nfifteen times the state,
Dialogue: 0,0:58:15.79,0:58:20.24,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}is an estimated six gigabytes\Nat two hundred fifty-six cubed.
Dialogue: 0,0:58:21.27,0:58:25.67,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Open one jar: one simulation,\None file, laid out like The Well,
Dialogue: 0,0:58:25.75,0:58:29.00,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}a reference dataset for machine learning.
Dialogue: 0,0:58:29.35,0:58:32.40,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Inside: the {\c&HE58739&}velocity{\c&HF7F4F2&}\Nand the magnetic field,
Dialogue: 0,0:58:32.48,0:58:37.72,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}snapshot after snapshot, and time\Nseries of energies and divergences.
Dialogue: 0,0:58:38.02,0:58:41.93,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}The file carries a unique\Nidentifier and the code's commit.
Dialogue: 0,0:58:42.67,0:58:46.15,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}The catalog repeats that\Nidentifier and adds a checksum.
Dialogue: 0,0:58:46.28,0:58:50.39,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}So an offline cluster can rebuild\Nits rows from the files alone.
Dialogue: 0,0:58:50.57,0:58:55.45,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Checksummed, not sealed: a forced\Nrewrite updates the checksum.
Dialogue: 0,0:58:56.50,0:59:01.11,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Follow one campaign down the line.\NOne command reads the recipe:
Dialogue: 0,0:59:01.19,0:59:04.23,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}the flat sheet, eleven {\c&H7AAF1B&}diffusivities{\c&HF7F4F2&}.
Dialogue: 0,0:59:04.46,0:59:07.35,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}The launcher opens one task per setting;
Dialogue: 0,0:59:07.43,0:59:12.35,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}each integrates sixteen runs\Nside by side and writes one file.
Dialogue: 0,0:59:12.73,0:59:18.25,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Each task adds a ledger row:\Nits status, checksum and wall time.
Dialogue: 0,0:59:18.99,0:59:21.92,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}This campaign has not run yet. The bet:
Dialogue: 0,0:59:22.00,0:59:26.49,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}all eleven tasks finish,\Neach with a checksummed file.
Dialogue: 0,0:59:26.98,0:59:32.18,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Confidence is high: small test\Ncampaigns already finished, in seconds.
Dialogue: 0,0:59:32.64,0:59:37.19,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}The report's own data, from the same\Nsolver, took about half an hour.
Dialogue: 0,0:59:39.84,0:59:43.21,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}A new instrument for the\N{\c&H00A1ED&}critic{\c&HF7F4F2&}: in the flat sheet,
Dialogue: 0,0:59:43.29,0:59:46.47,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}{\c&H3468EB&}field lines{\c&HF7F4F2&} are contours of one potential.
Dialogue: 0,0:59:46.81,0:59:50.64,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Lines loop around O-points\Nand cross at X-points.
Dialogue: 0,0:59:51.09,0:59:55.04,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}On this periodic square,\Nwhich kind is more common?
Dialogue: 0,0:59:58.28,1:00:03.58,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Neither: exactly as many of each,\Na free check on any counter.
Dialogue: 0,1:00:04.32,1:00:09.04,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}The internship offer asks for\Ntopology; the report computed none.
Dialogue: 0,1:00:09.27,1:00:13.92,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}A low-confidence bet: generated\Nfields match the simulated count.
Dialogue: 0,1:00:14.18,1:00:17.66,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}The plan lists the count,\Nbut sets no pass mark.
Dialogue: 0,1:00:19.60,1:00:22.88,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Which topology metric should\Njudge generated fields,
Dialogue: 0,1:00:22.96,1:00:26.19,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}and what gap in X-point\Ncounts fails a model?
Dialogue: 0,1:00:28.64,1:00:32.37,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Before trusting the solver,\Ntest it on a known answer.
Dialogue: 0,1:00:32.68,1:00:37.30,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}The twisting {\c&HE58739&}flow{\c&HF7F4F2&} offers one:\Na field shaped exactly like it.
Dialogue: 0,1:00:37.61,1:00:42.80,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Its lines follow the flow's own lines,\Nso carrying and stretching cancel.
Dialogue: 0,1:00:43.09,1:00:49.09,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Only diffusion acts: its growth\Nrate is exactly minus one over {\c&H7AAF1B&}Rm{\c&HF7F4F2&}.
Dialogue: 0,1:00:49.86,1:00:52.99,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}So the measured rate, the slowest fade,
Dialogue: 0,1:00:53.07,1:00:56.95,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}can be no lower:\Nat {\c&H7AAF1B&}Rm{\c&HF7F4F2&} two, minus one half.
Dialogue: 0,1:00:57.32,1:01:00.93,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}The solver finds minus zero\Npoint five zero zero one,
Dialogue: 0,1:01:01.01,1:01:04.62,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}on the bound to about\None part in ten thousand.
Dialogue: 0,1:01:05.67,1:01:08.70,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Run long enough, and one {\c&H3468EB&}field{\c&HF7F4F2&} takes over:
Dialogue: 0,1:01:08.78,1:01:12.36,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}the slowest to fade,\Nor the fastest to grow.
Dialogue: 0,1:01:12.81,1:01:17.57,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}The surface wraps its strong\Nparts; threads trace its lines.
Dialogue: 0,1:01:18.31,1:01:20.61,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}At {\c&H7AAF1B&}Rm{\c&HF7F4F2&} eight, it fades.
Dialogue: 0,1:01:20.93,1:01:25.06,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}At twelve and sixteen,\Ninside the window, it grows.
Dialogue: 0,1:01:25.45,1:01:28.81,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}At twenty, just past it, it fades again.
Dialogue: 0,1:01:29.37,1:01:35.37,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}In these snapshots, at eight, sixteen and\Ntwenty, it gathers in four slim tubes.
Dialogue: 0,1:01:35.83,1:01:38.87,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}They peak at points where\Nthe {\c&HE58739&}flow{\c&HF7F4F2&} stands still.
Dialogue: 0,1:01:39.20,1:01:41.49,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}At twelve, it spreads out.
Dialogue: 0,1:01:42.23,1:01:45.28,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Published work describes\Nsuch cigar-shaped tubes,
Dialogue: 0,1:01:45.36,1:01:48.24,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}and symmetries the report did not check.
Dialogue: 0,1:01:48.56,1:01:54.15,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}One shape per setting: proposed first\Ntargets for a three-dimensional {\c&HF09CB4&}generator{\c&HF7F4F2&}.
Dialogue: 0,1:01:55.40,1:01:59.02,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}How precise are those edges?\NEach run gets a long fit
Dialogue: 0,1:01:59.10,1:02:03.27,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}over its second half, a late\Nfit over its last quarter.
Dialogue: 0,1:02:03.59,1:02:09.32,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}A finer grid rechecks two settings.\NAt {\c&H7AAF1B&}Rm{\c&HF7F4F2&} twelve, all three agree.
Dialogue: 0,1:02:10.06,1:02:13.98,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}At seventeen and a half,\Nright on the edge: same sign?
Dialogue: 0,1:02:17.22,1:02:22.70,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}No: the long fit says growing;\Nthe late fit and finer grid, fading.
Dialogue: 0,1:02:23.01,1:02:25.94,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}This edge is known to\Nabout zero point one;
Dialogue: 0,1:02:26.02,1:02:29.36,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}the third, near twenty-seven,\Nto about one.
Dialogue: 0,1:02:29.78,1:02:35.73,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}A likely cause, still unchecked: the modes\Noscillate, so the growth curve wobbles.
Dialogue: 0,1:02:35.92,1:02:39.08,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}The divergence, at least,\Nstays at rounding error.
Dialogue: 0,1:02:39.25,1:02:42.77,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}That unresolved sign is\Nwhere Bet one begins.
Dialogue: 0,1:02:43.90,1:02:46.58,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Near the thresholds,\Nshould we fit growth rates
Dialogue: 0,1:02:46.66,1:02:49.74,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}with an oscillation term,\Nor simply run longer?
Dialogue: 0,1:02:51.97,1:02:54.41,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Bet one: the twisting box.
Dialogue: 0,1:02:54.86,1:02:59.08,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}At thirty-two cubed, the small\Nsweep matched the published windows,
Dialogue: 0,1:02:59.16,1:03:03.78,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}but near seventeen and a half the\Nsign of growth stayed unresolved.
Dialogue: 0,1:03:04.52,1:03:09.71,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}The campaign doubles the resolution and\Nsteps {\c&H7AAF1B&}Rm{\c&HF7F4F2&} by whole numbers up to forty.
Dialogue: 0,1:03:10.27,1:03:13.33,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}The bet: growth still\Npositive at seventeen,
Dialogue: 0,1:03:13.41,1:03:17.11,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}already negative at eighteen,\Nwith two fits agreeing:
Dialogue: 0,1:03:17.19,1:03:21.87,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}one over the kept part of the run,\None over its last quarter.
Dialogue: 0,1:03:23.41,1:03:27.14,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Confidence: medium. At eighteen,\Nthe small sweep's two
Dialogue: 0,1:03:27.22,1:03:31.14,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}fits already differed by\Nmore than the rate itself.
Dialogue: 0,1:03:31.61,1:03:36.79,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Worth asking before the run: add three\Npoints between seventeen and eighteen?
Dialogue: 0,1:03:38.04,1:03:40.44,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Bet two, the first true dynamo:
Dialogue: 0,1:03:40.52,1:03:44.61,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}a Taylor-Green flow of\Ncounter-rotating eddies.
Dialogue: 0,1:03:45.16,1:03:47.68,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Now the {\c&H3468EB&}field{\c&HF7F4F2&} pushes back on the flow.
Dialogue: 0,1:03:47.96,1:03:52.90,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Above a threshold, a tiny {\c&H3468EB&}seed{\c&HF7F4F2&}\Nshould grow, then level off.
Dialogue: 0,1:03:53.64,1:03:58.05,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Twelve runs cross four Reynolds\Nnumbers with three values of {\c&H7AAF1B&}Pm{\c&HF7F4F2&}.
Dialogue: 0,1:03:58.65,1:04:04.55,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}The bet: a threshold in {\c&H7AAF1B&}Rm{\c&HF7F4F2&},\Nbracketed for at least two values of Pm.
Dialogue: 0,1:04:06.09,1:04:11.12,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Confidence: medium. Published runs\Ndefine the Reynolds number differently,
Dialogue: 0,1:04:11.20,1:04:14.30,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}so the threshold may fall off this grid.
Dialogue: 0,1:04:14.78,1:04:19.45,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Near threshold, a run may still be\Ngrowing when its snapshots are kept.
Dialogue: 0,1:04:19.94,1:04:23.51,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Lengthen those runs,\Nor flag and exclude them?
Dialogue: 0,1:04:25.14,1:04:30.67,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Near a gentle threshold, the truth rises\Nlike a square root, vertical at first.
Dialogue: 0,1:04:31.11,1:04:33.54,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}A stiff {\c&HF09CB4&}machine{\c&HF7F4F2&} rounds the corner;
Dialogue: 0,1:04:33.62,1:04:37.72,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}a supple one errs less,\Nbut turns jumpy everywhere.
Dialogue: 0,1:04:38.46,1:04:42.38,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Near a subcritical threshold,\Ntwo states coexist.
Dialogue: 0,1:04:42.85,1:04:46.35,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}In published Taylor-Green\Nruns, a dynamo, once on,
Dialogue: 0,1:04:46.43,1:04:49.77,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}survived down to a third of its threshold.
Dialogue: 0,1:04:50.25,1:04:53.66,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Told only the {\c&H7AAF1B&}knob{\c&HF7F4F2&}, a machine blends them.
Dialogue: 0,1:04:54.40,1:04:59.59,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}The plan's bet: tell it the branch,\Nand its threshold error at least halves.
Dialogue: 0,1:05:00.93,1:05:06.93,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Confidence: low; statistical stability\Nalong branches is unproven in turbulence.
Dialogue: 0,1:05:07.23,1:05:12.78,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}No downward sweep is configured: from\Nwhich saturated runs, and how far down?
Dialogue: 0,1:05:14.20,1:05:16.15,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Some things need no bet.
Dialogue: 0,1:05:16.58,1:05:19.65,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}In three dimensions,\Nhill maps become potentials:
Dialogue: 0,1:05:19.73,1:05:23.13,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}the {\c&HF09CB4&}generator{\c&HF7F4F2&} draws them,\Nthen takes their curl.
Dialogue: 0,1:05:23.30,1:05:26.92,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}So the {\c&HE58739&}flow{\c&HF7F4F2&} and the field stay\Ndivergence-free to rounding,
Dialogue: 0,1:05:27.00,1:05:29.08,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}even with untrained weights.
Dialogue: 0,1:05:29.82,1:05:34.43,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Mirror the starting noise, and the\Nsample comes out with its {\c&H3468EB&}field{\c&HF7F4F2&} flipped.
Dialogue: 0,1:05:34.89,1:05:37.92,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}That shows the mechanism, not the quality.
Dialogue: 0,1:05:38.97,1:05:40.88,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Everything else must be measured.
Dialogue: 0,1:05:41.33,1:05:44.90,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}First test: does the {\c&HF09CB4&}generator{\c&HF7F4F2&}\Nget the energy spectra right,
Dialogue: 0,1:05:44.98,1:05:48.84,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}within a tenth of a decade,\Nat the settings it trained on?
Dialogue: 0,1:05:49.58,1:05:51.37,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Confidence: low.
Dialogue: 0,1:05:51.77,1:05:55.64,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}In two dimensions, the best\Nmodels only just got there,
Dialogue: 0,1:05:55.72,1:06:01.43,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}and here each run keeps forty snapshots,\Nagainst five thousand in the plan.
Dialogue: 0,1:06:03.77,1:06:09.77,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Now the crowd law in three dimensions:\Nenergy in equals energy out, on average.
Dialogue: 0,1:06:10.41,1:06:13.93,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}In two dimensions, the crowd\Nloss cut the {\c&H00A1ED&}budget error{\c&HF7F4F2&}
Dialogue: 0,1:06:14.01,1:06:18.60,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}to six percent; an equal-length\Ncontrol run stayed at forty-six.
Dialogue: 0,1:06:19.34,1:06:22.48,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}The bet: under five percent\Nat held-out settings,
Dialogue: 0,1:06:22.56,1:06:26.84,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}the control higher,\Nthe dissipation's {\c&H00A1ED&}spread{\c&HF7F4F2&} within a fifth.
Dialogue: 0,1:06:28.18,1:06:32.08,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Confidence: low. On three\Ndials, the loss shrank the
Dialogue: 0,1:06:32.16,1:06:36.86,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}spread of energy changes,\Nand failed from a biased start.
Dialogue: 0,1:06:37.25,1:06:43.25,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Ask first: where runs may still be\Ngrowing, trust the data or the crowd law?
Dialogue: 0,1:06:44.80,1:06:48.74,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Before the Taylor-Green runs, which\Nforcing, Reynolds number definition
Dialogue: 0,1:06:48.82,1:06:52.94,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}and resolution make their threshold\Ncomparable with published ones?
Dialogue: 0,1:06:55.17,1:06:59.38,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Two steps remain: the VKS\Ncylinder with its iron impellers,
Dialogue: 0,1:06:59.46,1:07:02.34,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}and DRESDYN, a precessing cylinder.
Dialogue: 0,1:07:02.95,1:07:08.95,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}First, what exact means once there are\Nwalls, metals and meshes; then their bets.
Dialogue: 0,1:07:12.07,1:07:14.44,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Before the walls, one convention:
Dialogue: 0,1:07:14.52,1:07:18.85,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}in the sodium experiment,\N{\c&H7AAF1B&}Rm{\c&HF7F4F2&} has no single definition.
Dialogue: 0,1:07:19.17,1:07:23.90,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}One paper multiplies by an impeller\Nefficiency near zero point six,
Dialogue: 0,1:07:23.98,1:07:27.39,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}another does not,\Na third uses other radii.
Dialogue: 0,1:07:28.13,1:07:33.14,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}So one threshold reads about thirty-one,\Nor fifty-two to seventy-one.
Dialogue: 0,1:07:33.51,1:07:37.60,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}A {\c&HF09CB4&}generator{\c&HF7F4F2&} conditioned on\NRm must say which it means.
Dialogue: 0,1:07:38.64,1:07:39.93,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Now the walls.
Dialogue: 0,1:07:40.26,1:07:42.64,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Many potentials draw the same {\c&H3468EB&}field{\c&HF7F4F2&}:
Dialogue: 0,1:07:42.72,1:07:46.67,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}add the slope of any hill,\Nand the curl is unchanged.
Dialogue: 0,1:07:47.14,1:07:51.65,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Choose among them without a fixed rule,\Nand the {\c&HF09CB4&}generator{\c&HF7F4F2&} learns noise.
Dialogue: 0,1:07:52.39,1:07:58.39,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}So fix the gauge: no divergence inside,\Nno potential flowing through the wall.
Dialogue: 0,1:07:58.81,1:08:02.04,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}A published theorem then\Ngives each admissible
Dialogue: 0,1:08:02.12,1:08:06.12,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}{\c&H3468EB&}field{\c&HF7F4F2&} one potential: none\Nlost, none counted twice.
Dialogue: 0,1:08:06.49,1:08:08.54,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}If the {\c&H3468EB&}field{\c&HF7F4F2&} never crosses the wall,
Dialogue: 0,1:08:08.62,1:08:11.92,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}the potential may instead\Nmeet it at right angles.
Dialogue: 0,1:08:12.66,1:08:16.61,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}One {\c&H00A1ED&}metric{\c&HF7F4F2&} changes meaning:\Nwhere the field crosses the wall,
Dialogue: 0,1:08:16.69,1:08:21.85,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}magnetic helicity depends on the gauge,\Nso a relative helicity is needed.
Dialogue: 0,1:08:22.87,1:08:25.47,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Now the iron that made VKS work.
Dialogue: 0,1:08:25.84,1:08:29.55,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}At its surface, the part of the\N{\c&H3468EB&}field{\c&HF7F4F2&} crossing it is continuous,
Dialogue: 0,1:08:29.63,1:08:33.33,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}and so is the part along it,\Ndivided by the permeability.
Dialogue: 0,1:08:34.07,1:08:37.07,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Can a smooth potential obey both rules?
Dialogue: 0,1:08:40.31,1:08:43.56,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}No: a smooth potential\Nkeeps the {\c&H3468EB&}field{\c&HF7F4F2&} continuous,
Dialogue: 0,1:08:43.64,1:08:48.29,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}breaking the second rule unless\Nthe {\c&H3468EB&}field{\c&HF7F4F2&} meets the iron head-on.
Dialogue: 0,1:08:48.71,1:08:50.91,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}The plan proposes an untested fix:
Dialogue: 0,1:08:50.99,1:08:54.46,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}a potential deliberately\Nkinked at the surface.
Dialogue: 0,1:08:55.20,1:08:58.83,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Outside, the {\c&H3468EB&}field{\c&HF7F4F2&} must\Njoin a current-free field:
Dialogue: 0,1:08:58.91,1:09:04.07,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}linear but nonlocal, each wall\Npoint depending on the whole wall.
Dialogue: 0,1:09:04.44,1:09:06.25,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}How to impose it is open.
Dialogue: 0,1:09:07.30,1:09:10.96,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}In the periodic box, two scalars,\Npoloidal and toroidal,
Dialogue: 0,1:09:11.04,1:09:14.24,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}can replace a potential's\Nthree components.
Dialogue: 0,1:09:14.70,1:09:17.82,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Their {\c&H3468EB&}field{\c&HF7F4F2&} is divergence-free,\Nwith no gauge:
Dialogue: 0,1:09:17.90,1:09:20.86,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}the plan stores its box data that way.
Dialogue: 0,1:09:21.60,1:09:27.60,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}The partners' solver slices space instead:\Naround the axis, waves of zero,
Dialogue: 0,1:09:27.69,1:09:33.69,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}one, two, three bumps per turn;\Nacross each half-plane, a triangle mesh.
Dialogue: 0,1:09:34.68,1:09:40.36,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}A continuous potential on that mesh gives\Na divergence-free curl, wave by wave.
Dialogue: 0,1:09:40.86,1:09:43.96,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}But the solver's data are\Nonly nearly divergence-free:
Dialogue: 0,1:09:44.04,1:09:46.95,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}the {\c&HF09CB4&}generator{\c&HF7F4F2&} will be\Ncleaner than its data.
Dialogue: 0,1:09:47.17,1:09:49.39,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Measure with the solver's yardstick.
Dialogue: 0,1:09:51.84,1:09:54.11,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}The VKS bet would train a {\c&HF09CB4&}generator{\c&HF7F4F2&} on the
Dialogue: 0,1:09:54.19,1:09:57.15,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}partners' simulations of\Nthe sodium experiment.
Dialogue: 0,1:09:57.55,1:10:01.13,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}In their reference run,\Nfour patterns dominate:
Dialogue: 0,1:10:01.21,1:10:05.13,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}a dipole, a quadrupole,\Nand two {\c&HE58739&}flow{\c&HF7F4F2&} patterns.
Dialogue: 0,1:10:05.87,1:10:08.81,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}The bet: their energy\Nshares within ten percent,
Dialogue: 0,1:10:08.89,1:10:10.64,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}and the {\c&H00A1ED&}distribution distance{\c&HF7F4F2&}
Dialogue: 0,1:10:10.72,1:10:14.40,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}of dipole and quadrupole\Nunder twice the {\c&H00A1ED&}noise floor{\c&HF7F4F2&}.
Dialogue: 0,1:10:14.74,1:10:17.14,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Plus the iron rule within one percent,
Dialogue: 0,1:10:17.22,1:10:21.79,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}and Rice's reversal rate inside\Nthe simulated count's error bars.
Dialogue: 0,1:10:23.03,1:10:26.11,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Confidence: low. The data are not public,
Dialogue: 0,1:10:26.19,1:10:31.61,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}no file reader exists yet, and few\Nreversals mean wide error bars.
Dialogue: 0,1:10:32.07,1:10:37.33,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Worth asking first: how many snapshots\Nper configuration, and by when?
Dialogue: 0,1:10:38.77,1:10:44.50,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}The DRESDYN bet: a sodium cylinder\Nspinning while its axis slowly turns.
Dialogue: 0,1:10:44.87,1:10:50.76,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Its {\c&H7AAF1B&}knob{\c&HF7F4F2&}: the precession ratio, how fast\Nthe axis turns relative to the spin.
Dialogue: 0,1:10:51.50,1:10:54.60,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}The {\c&HF09CB4&}generator{\c&HF7F4F2&} draws only\Nthe flow; a solver finds the
Dialogue: 0,1:10:54.68,1:10:58.67,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}threshold and the symmetry of\Nthe {\c&H3468EB&}field{\c&HF7F4F2&} that flow would grow.
Dialogue: 0,1:10:58.94,1:11:04.85,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}The bet: both right at an unseen ratio,\Nthe threshold within ten percent.
Dialogue: 0,1:11:06.09,1:11:11.79,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Confidence: low. Few ratios are planned,\Nand this step would be dropped first.
Dialogue: 0,1:11:12.15,1:11:16.68,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Worth asking now: how many ratios,\Nand how close together?
Dialogue: 0,1:11:18.14,1:11:21.18,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Downstream, the {\c&HF09CB4&}generator{\c&HF7F4F2&}\Nfeeds reduced models:
Dialogue: 0,1:11:21.26,1:11:25.24,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}their best drift averages the\Nequations' drift over snapshots
Dialogue: 0,1:11:25.32,1:11:28.78,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}sharing a reduced state,\Nno time series needed.
Dialogue: 0,1:11:29.52,1:11:31.46,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}It also serves as a prior.
Dialogue: 0,1:11:31.73,1:11:35.61,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}A probe reads the dipole\Nnear zero, mid-reversal.
Dialogue: 0,1:11:36.19,1:11:38.98,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Where does the average of\Nthe matching states sit?
Dialogue: 0,1:11:42.22,1:11:46.38,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Between two mirror humps,\Nwhere the dynamo almost never is.
Dialogue: 0,1:11:46.90,1:11:50.85,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Methods keeping only a mean\Nand a spread chase that ghost;
Dialogue: 0,1:11:50.93,1:11:54.54,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}a lopsided prior would\Nwrongly favour one hump.
Dialogue: 0,1:11:55.04,1:11:58.96,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}A built-in mirror handles it\Nexactly: sample mirror pairs,
Dialogue: 0,1:11:59.04,1:12:01.92,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}or search from a code and its mirror.
Dialogue: 0,1:12:03.27,1:12:08.17,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Which labels must each VKS snapshot\Ncarry: which Rm definition,
Dialogue: 0,1:12:08.25,1:12:12.22,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}which wall condition,\Nwhich iron permeability?
Dialogue: 0,1:12:15.67,1:12:18.86,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}The bill: about eleven\Nthousand graphics-card hours,
Dialogue: 0,1:12:18.94,1:12:22.41,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}counting a twenty percent\Nreserve for failed runs.
Dialogue: 0,1:12:22.87,1:12:25.82,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}The three-dimensional box\Ntakes the biggest share.
Dialogue: 0,1:12:26.16,1:12:29.36,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Most lines are scaled from\None published training run:
Dialogue: 0,1:12:29.44,1:12:32.02,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}about eleven hundred and fifty hours.
Dialogue: 0,1:12:32.76,1:12:35.21,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}No graphics-card hour\Nhas been measured yet.
Dialogue: 0,1:12:35.80,1:12:40.39,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}The bet: measured costs land\Nwithin a factor of two of the plan.
Dialogue: 0,1:12:41.63,1:12:47.11,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Confidence: low; the plan's own\Nerror bar is at least twofold.
Dialogue: 0,1:12:47.50,1:12:51.49,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Ask first: the reference\Nsimulations, twenty thousand
Dialogue: 0,1:12:51.57,1:12:56.66,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}processor hours or more, are off\Nthis bill. On whose machines?
Dialogue: 0,1:12:58.90,1:13:04.90,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}The whole plan on one page:\Nthirty-six months, three axes, five steps.
Dialogue: 0,1:13:05.09,1:13:08.98,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Hard constraints, crowd laws,\Nthresholds and reversals,
Dialogue: 0,1:13:09.06,1:13:12.41,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}from three dials to a precessing cylinder.
Dialogue: 0,1:13:12.63,1:13:15.90,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Three papers, at months\Nten, twenty-two and
Dialogue: 0,1:13:15.98,1:13:19.58,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}thirty-three, with no acceptance promised.
Dialogue: 0,1:13:20.32,1:13:23.37,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}It merges the two best-scored\Nof six directions;
Dialogue: 0,1:13:23.45,1:13:27.03,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}they stay on top under all\Nthree weightings tried.
Dialogue: 0,1:13:27.59,1:13:32.10,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Among its named risks:\Nnon-public data, a bill over budget,
Dialogue: 0,1:13:32.18,1:13:37.95,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}an unstable crowd loss in three\Ndimensions, the error near thresholds.
Dialogue: 0,1:13:38.12,1:13:43.24,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}One risk it cannot yet rate:\Nfunding after MilaDy's nominal end.
Dialogue: 0,1:13:44.38,1:13:49.28,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}An independent proposal, not requested\Nor validated by the supervisors.
Dialogue: 0,1:13:50.84,1:13:54.45,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}A thesis starting in fall\Ntwenty twenty-seven would end
Dialogue: 0,1:13:54.53,1:13:58.49,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}after MilaDy's nominal end:\Nwho funds its last months?
Dialogue: 0,1:14:03.54,1:14:06.46,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Every chapter so far\Nhas ended on a question.
Dialogue: 0,1:14:06.79,1:14:08.92,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}The empty seats raised more.
Dialogue: 0,1:14:09.66,1:14:12.22,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Hang each one under the step it shapes.
Dialogue: 0,1:14:12.69,1:14:17.11,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Bet one: whether to add settings\Nbetween seventeen and eighteen.
Dialogue: 0,1:14:17.62,1:14:22.66,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Bet two: the {\c&H3468EB&}seed{\c&HF7F4F2&}, the Reynolds number,\Nthe length of each run.
Dialogue: 0,1:14:22.95,1:14:25.34,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}The {\c&HF09CB4&}generator{\c&HF7F4F2&}: what it must match.
Dialogue: 0,1:14:25.56,1:14:28.18,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}The top steps: the partners' data.
Dialogue: 0,1:14:28.92,1:14:32.11,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}A staircase of bets is a\Nstaircase of decisions,
Dialogue: 0,1:14:32.19,1:14:35.18,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}and the first ones come before any run.
Dialogue: 0,1:14:37.00,1:14:41.44,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}First, the runs: these answers\Nchange what gets computed.
Dialogue: 0,1:14:42.18,1:14:47.64,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Near a threshold, start from a\Ntiny {\c&H3468EB&}seed{\c&HF7F4F2&}, a strong one, or both?
Dialogue: 0,1:14:48.21,1:14:51.93,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Which values of {\c&H7AAF1B&}Pm{\c&HF7F4F2&}, since\Nsodium's is out of reach?
Dialogue: 0,1:14:52.15,1:14:55.78,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Settings spread evenly,\Nor packed near each threshold?
Dialogue: 0,1:14:56.26,1:14:58.80,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}How many independent\Nsnapshots per setting?
Dialogue: 0,1:14:59.23,1:15:02.24,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}How long must runs near a threshold last?
Dialogue: 0,1:15:02.92,1:15:05.91,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Which forcing, Reynolds\Nnumber and grid make the
Dialogue: 0,1:15:05.99,1:15:09.76,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Taylor-Green threshold\Ncomparable with published ones?
Dialogue: 0,1:15:10.14,1:15:12.25,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}And which campaign comes first?
Dialogue: 0,1:15:13.30,1:15:19.18,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Then the framing: how each result will\Nbe judged, decided before the data exist.
Dialogue: 0,1:15:19.60,1:15:23.61,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Which statistics must generated\Nfields match, down to the shape of
Dialogue: 0,1:15:23.69,1:15:28.35,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}their field lines, and how close to\Nthe {\c&H00A1ED&}noise floor{\c&HF7F4F2&} counts as success?
Dialogue: 0,1:15:28.86,1:15:33.36,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Which budgets must hold {\c&H00A1ED&}on average{\c&HF7F4F2&},\Nand must their spread match too?
Dialogue: 0,1:15:34.40,1:15:39.10,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}One answer, not about physics,\Ncould make most renting unnecessary.
Dialogue: 0,1:15:39.45,1:15:40.76,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Which question is it?
Dialogue: 0,1:15:44.00,1:15:45.70,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Where the computing happens.
Dialogue: 0,1:15:46.11,1:15:50.38,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}If a lab has graphics cards an\Nintern can use, or there is already
Dialogue: 0,1:15:50.46,1:15:54.80,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}a Jean Zay project to join,\Nrenting shrinks to a short trial.
Dialogue: 0,1:15:55.15,1:16:01.13,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}And for the top steps: which partner\Nsimulations, in what form, and by when?
Dialogue: 0,1:16:02.17,1:16:04.82,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}The second shelf matters\Nfor the internship plan
Dialogue: 0,1:16:04.90,1:16:08.08,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}and the data request,\Nnot for the first campaigns.
Dialogue: 0,1:16:08.69,1:16:12.02,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Which settings get runs long\Nenough to count reversals?
Dialogue: 0,1:16:12.34,1:16:15.57,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Which operator decides what exact means?
Dialogue: 0,1:16:15.78,1:16:18.48,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Which labels must each snapshot carry?
Dialogue: 0,1:16:18.75,1:16:21.26,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}And may new results appear in this film?
Dialogue: 0,1:16:22.00,1:16:28.00,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}The last shelf can wait: the top steps'\Ndetails, and how to frame a first paper.
Dialogue: 0,1:16:29.14,1:16:33.94,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}The first shelf fits in one message,\Nto write before renting anything.
Dialogue: 0,1:16:34.37,1:16:36.49,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}This ranking is only a proposal.
Dialogue: 0,1:16:36.70,1:16:38.83,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}The supervisors may well change it.
Dialogue: 0,1:16:39.57,1:16:41.93,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Several answers could\Nchange what gets computed,
Dialogue: 0,1:16:42.01,1:16:44.22,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}or whether anything is rented at all.
Dialogue: 0,1:16:44.59,1:16:46.99,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}The cheapest experiment\Nis a good question,
Dialogue: 0,1:16:47.07,1:16:49.54,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}asked before renting a graphics card.
Dialogue: 0,1:16:54.97,1:16:57.28,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Questions first, then computing.
Dialogue: 0,1:16:57.55,1:17:00.86,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Here is what those questions\Nrest on, in two columns.
Dialogue: 0,1:17:01.23,1:17:06.19,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}On the left, what three small experiments\Nmeasured on an ordinary processor.
Dialogue: 0,1:17:06.36,1:17:08.70,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Toy problems, one or two training seeds,
Dialogue: 0,1:17:08.78,1:17:13.17,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}and machines tested only between\Nthe settings they were trained on.
Dialogue: 0,1:17:13.91,1:17:18.89,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}On the right, what the staircase bets,\Neach with the rule that will settle it.
Dialogue: 0,1:17:19.67,1:17:23.33,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Each lamp shows a verdict only\Nonce its data file arrives,
Dialogue: 0,1:17:23.41,1:17:25.81,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}and the film is updated with it.
Dialogue: 0,1:17:27.44,1:17:30.35,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Back to the compass,\Nand to the two questions.
Dialogue: 0,1:17:31.59,1:17:33.36,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}When will this needle turn?
Dialogue: 0,1:17:33.69,1:17:36.00,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}For the Earth, no one can say.
Dialogue: 0,1:17:36.52,1:17:39.18,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}In a simple computer\Nmodel, chaos makes the
Dialogue: 0,1:17:39.26,1:17:42.34,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}forecast fade before\Nthe average wait ends.
Dialogue: 0,1:17:42.75,1:17:48.42,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}But {\c&H00A1ED&}how often{\c&HF7F4F2&} the field flips, counted as\Ncrossings of zero, can still be answered.
Dialogue: 0,1:17:48.93,1:17:53.76,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}The answer hides in a shoebox of\Nsnapshots, read with the equations.
Dialogue: 0,1:17:54.38,1:17:59.97,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}And a {\c&HF09CB4&}machine{\c&HF7F4F2&} can learn to dream such\Nsnapshots, between the simulated settings.
Dialogue: 0,1:18:01.11,1:18:04.01,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}On small test problems, it partly works.
Dialogue: 0,1:18:04.28,1:18:07.41,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}The mirror holds by construction,\Nat every setting.
Dialogue: 0,1:18:07.69,1:18:10.27,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}On three dials, the {\c&H00A1ED&}rate of zero crossings{\c&HF7F4F2&}
Dialogue: 0,1:18:10.35,1:18:14.36,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}is roughly right far from the\Nthreshold, and poor near it.
Dialogue: 0,1:18:14.66,1:18:17.93,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}A smooth machine rounds\Noff a steep change.
Dialogue: 0,1:18:18.11,1:18:21.04,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}The bigger tests,\Nin three dimensions and in the
Dialogue: 0,1:18:21.12,1:18:24.27,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}shapes of real machines,\Nare written as bets.
Dialogue: 0,1:18:25.61,1:18:27.15,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}One lesson remains.
Dialogue: 0,1:18:27.37,1:18:29.95,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Enforce each law where it is true.
Dialogue: 0,1:18:30.22,1:18:32.77,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Exactly, where it is exact.
Dialogue: 0,1:18:33.06,1:18:36.60,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}{\c&H00A1ED&}On average{\c&HF7F4F2&}, where it\Nonly holds on average.
Dialogue: 0,1:18:40.24,1:18:43.42,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}The report, the code,\Nand every result in this film are
Dialogue: 0,1:18:43.50,1:18:46.97,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}on the project's site,\Nwith their limits written out.
Dialogue: 0,1:18:47.46,1:18:50.37,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}The rest of the staircase\Nis still to be computed.
